#!/usr/bin/env bash
# iso/bin/build -- the T5 fork's single build entry point.
#
# docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §1: "bin/build does, in order: verify the
# submodule is exactly at UPSTREAM (refuse otherwise); rsync overlay/ over a
# scratch copy of it; git apply --3way the patches, failing loudly on any
# reject; run the guards in §6; then omarchy-iso-make's work with the three
# §3.10 fixes intact."
#
# The overlay is still EMPTY (T5a) -- no configs/airootfs content, no patches.
# T5a's job was to prove the pipeline reproduces the known-good ISO before
# anything gets added on top (§7: "Do not start T5c before T5a proves
# parity").
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# T5b: the runtime pin is now real, and guard 6.4 enforces the coupling
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# iso/RUNTIME existed before this slice but nothing read it. That is the
# hole docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §0 is about: the git ref and the package
# channel are TWO independent pins, and only one of them is expressible in
# git. `OMARCHY_MIRROR=edge` is a rolling channel with no history -- it
# served omarchy-dev @6d7826d on 2026-08-10 and @1c9dfc5 the next day, and
# the rename in between (`omarchy-setup-system` -> `omarchy-apply-system`)
# means an ISO forked at our UPSTREAM pin but built against today's edge
# calls a binary the runtime no longer ships. It dies at phase 5 of 14, after
# partitioning, LUKS, pacstrap and ~1200 packages.
#
# Two mechanisms close it, and this slice adds both:
#
#   1. --local-source (§2 mechanism A). Upstream's own supported path:
#      builder/build-omarchy-packages.sh builds omarchy-dev,
#      omarchy-settings-dev and omarchy-nvim from mounted git checkouts
#      straight into the offline mirror, and build-iso.sh then EXCLUDES those
#      three from the network `pacman -Syw`. So the runtime the ISO carries
#      comes from iso/RUNTIME's sha rather than from whatever the channel
#      served this morning. It is no longer optional here: a build that falls
#      back to the channel would have an unpinned runtime, which is the
#      defect this slice exists to remove.
#
#      ⚠️ It pins only the three omarchy-* packages. Arch's core/extra/
#      multilib still roll through mirror.omarchy.org (§2). "Reproducible"
#      here means "same inputs by declaration", not bit-identical.
#
#   2. Guard 6.4 (§6.4), in two halves, because neither alone is honest:
#
#      6.4a  BEFORE docker: every /usr/bin/omarchy-* our (patched)
#            orchestrator shells out to exists in the pinned runtime
#            checkout's bin/. Necessary, not sufficient -- the PKGBUILD
#            decides what actually gets installed -- but it costs a grep and
#            it refuses the build before six gigabytes of downloads.
#      6.4b  AFTER docker, on the .pkg.tar.zst files the build actually
#            produced: the same names exist as usr/bin/<name> members of
#            omarchy-dev, omarchy-settings-dev, or one of OUR OWN locally
#            built packages (configs/deck/pkgbuilds/*). This is the
#            sufficient half. They are checked as a UNION because that is
#            how they install: omarchy-dev's PKGBUILD ships all of bin/
#            EXCEPT omarchy-debug, omarchy-debug-idle and omarchy-upload-log,
#            which omarchy-settings-dev ships instead, and omarchy-dev
#            hard-depends on omarchy-settings-dev; and omarchy-deck ships
#            /usr/bin/omarchy-deck-apply-patches, which the pinned runtime
#            will never contain. The union keeps the sets DISTINGUISHABLE:
#            "the pinned runtime does not ship this" and "our package does
#            not ship this either" are different bugs with different fixes,
#            and both halves say which one they mean.
#
#      6.4b also asserts the pin TOOK EFFECT: both PKGBUILDs derive pkgver
#      as <base>.r<count>.g<short sha> from the checkout's git history, so a
#      built package whose version does not carry iso/RUNTIME's short sha was
#      not built from the tree we pinned -- whatever else went right.
#
# The name list is DERIVED (grepped out of the scratch tree's orchestrator
# after our patches are applied), never hard-coded, so it cannot go stale
# against our own edits -- §6.4's requirement.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this does not just exec upstream's own bin/omarchy-iso-make
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# omarchy-iso-make (iso/upstream/bin/omarchy-iso-make) does two things this
# project's CLAUDE.md forbids doing to the DEV MACHINE, unconditionally, on
# every invocation:
#
#   1. `sudo rm -rf /var/cache/pacman/pkg/*` on the HOST, before Docker even
#      starts. On this dev machine that cache holds 2700+ packages
#      (docs/PROGRESS.md §3.10 item 3 / §7). There is no flag to skip this.
#   2. Bind-mounts the (now-emptied) host cache read-write into a --privileged
#      container.
#
# That is guard 6.3 (docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §6.3): "Do not inherit the
# pacman-cache rm -rf... bin/build points the container at a scratch dir
# under iso/.cache/ instead -- same rebuild speed, no blast radius." The only
# way to get that is to stop calling omarchy-iso-make and drive
# builder/build-iso.sh's Docker invocation ourselves, keeping everything else
# (image, --privileged, the env vars build-iso.sh reads) identical.
#
# Guard 6.1 (ref/mirror agreement) is handled the same way: hard-coded here,
# with the environment refused rather than silently overridden if it disagrees.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where things land
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Never in this repo. All scratch state -- the overlaid upstream checkout,
# the pacman package cache, the offline-mirror download cache, and the built
# ISO itself -- lives under $OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR (default
# ~/.cache/omarchy-deck/iso-build), a sibling of the repo, not a subdirectory
# of it. Multi-gigabyte artifacts belong outside version control
# (docs/PROGRESS.md §1.1) and outside this worktree.
#
# Usage:
#   iso/bin/build
#   OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR=/some/scratch/dir iso/bin/build
#
# Env vars:
#   OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR   scratch root (default: see above)
#   OMARCHY_INSTALL_DEBUG        passed through to build-iso.sh unchanged
#   OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC      path to an existing basecamp/omarchy
#                                 checkout. Default: a clone this script
#                                 manages under the scratch root, checked out
#                                 at iso/RUNTIME's sha. When you point this at
#                                 a checkout of your own it is VERIFIED, never
#                                 moved: a HEAD that is not iso/RUNTIME's pin,
#                                 or a dirty tree, is refused. Your checkout is
#                                 an input to the packages this builds
#                                 (makepkg copies the whole tree, untracked
#                                 files included), so "dirty" really is
#                                 "unreproducible", not pedantry.
#   OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC         path to an existing omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs
#                                 checkout -- the PKGBUILDs. REQUIRED unless
#                                 iso/PKGS exists, because this repo does not
#                                 yet pin that third input; see step 2b.
#   OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_NO_NETWORK_HOST
#                                 unset by default. build-iso.sh downloads
#                                 hundreds of packages; Docker's default
#                                 bridge network is throttled to ~2 KB/s on
#                                 this dev machine (docs/PROGRESS.md §7), so
#                                 `docker run --network host` is the default.
#                                 Set this to 1 to fall back to Docker's
#                                 normal bridge network instead.
#
# Exit codes: 0 success. Any failure below is fail-loud (set -euo pipefail
# plus explicit checks) -- CLAUDE.md: "never silently swallow a failure."

set -euo pipefail

BUILD_PROG=iso-build
build_log()  { printf '[%s] %s\n' "$BUILD_PROG" "$*"; }
build_fail() { printf '[%s] ERROR: %s\n' "$BUILD_PROG" "$*" >&2; exit 1; }

ISO_ROOT=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)
REPO_ROOT=$(cd -- "$ISO_ROOT/.." && pwd)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pin helpers. Three inputs want the same treatment (iso/UPSTREAM, iso/RUNTIME
# and -- if it ever exists -- iso/PKGS), and one implementation of "is this
# checkout exactly at its pin?" is the only way all three stay equally strict.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# parse_pin <file> -- sets PIN_SLUG and PIN_SHA from a one-line 'owner/repo@sha'.
PIN_SLUG=""
PIN_SHA=""
parse_pin() {
  local file=$1 line
  [[ -f $file ]] || build_fail "missing $file"
  line=$(head -n1 "$file")
  [[ $line =~ ^([^/]+/[^@]+)@([0-9a-f]{7,40})$ ]] ||
    build_fail "$file does not look like 'owner/repo@sha': '$line'"
  PIN_SLUG=${BASH_REMATCH[1]}
  PIN_SHA=${BASH_REMATCH[2]}
}

# verify_checkout_at_pin <dir> <sha> <label> <remedy> -- sets CHECKOUT_HEAD.
#
# Sets a global rather than echoing its result: build_fail's `exit 1` inside a
# command substitution would only kill the subshell, and relying on set -e to
# turn that into a real exit is exactly the kind of "fails, but only if you
# read it right" construct this project keeps getting bitten by.
#
# Refuses anything that is not a clean git checkout sitting exactly on <sha>.
# Deliberately NOT "checkout it for us": when the caller supplied the path, a
# disagreement is a disagreement -- guard 6.1's shape (§6.1) -- and silently
# moving someone's working tree onto a different commit is the same class of
# error as silently overriding their OMARCHY_MIRROR. Prints the resolved HEAD
# so a green line is evidence, not reassurance.
CHECKOUT_HEAD=""
verify_checkout_at_pin() {
  local dir=$1 sha=$2 label=$3 remedy=$4 dirty
  CHECKOUT_HEAD=""
  [[ -e "$dir/.git" ]] || build_fail "$label: $dir is not a git checkout. $remedy"
  CHECKOUT_HEAD=$(git -C "$dir" rev-parse HEAD) ||
    build_fail "$label: could not read HEAD of $dir"
  case $CHECKOUT_HEAD in
    "$sha"*) ;;
    *) build_fail "$label: $dir is at $CHECKOUT_HEAD, but the pin is $sha. Refusing to build from an unpinned checkout -- $remedy" ;;
  esac
  dirty=$(git -C "$dir" status --porcelain)
  [[ -z $dirty ]] ||
    build_fail "$label: $dir has local modifications, so it is not 'exactly at the pin'. These files go into the build (makepkg copies the whole tree, untracked included), which makes the result unreproducible:"$'\n'"$dirty"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Verify the submodule is exactly at UPSTREAM. Refuse otherwise.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

parse_pin "$ISO_ROOT/UPSTREAM"
UPSTREAM_SLUG=$PIN_SLUG
UPSTREAM_PIN=$PIN_SHA

UPSTREAM_DIR="$ISO_ROOT/upstream"
[[ -e "$UPSTREAM_DIR/.git" ]] ||
  build_fail "$UPSTREAM_DIR is not a checked-out submodule. Run: git -C '$REPO_ROOT' submodule update --init iso/upstream"

verify_checkout_at_pin "$UPSTREAM_DIR" "$UPSTREAM_PIN" "iso/upstream" \
  "run 'git -C iso/upstream checkout $UPSTREAM_PIN' (or update iso/UPSTREAM if the pin is meant to move) and re-run."
upstream_head=$CHECKOUT_HEAD

build_log "upstream pin OK: $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1b. Read the OTHER pin -- the one the channel cannot express.
#
# Parsed here (beside UPSTREAM) so a malformed iso/RUNTIME is refused in the
# same breath as a malformed iso/UPSTREAM. The checkouts themselves are
# materialised in step 2b, once the scratch root exists.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

parse_pin "$ISO_ROOT/RUNTIME"
RUNTIME_SLUG=$PIN_SLUG
RUNTIME_PIN=$PIN_SHA
build_log "runtime pin: $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_PIN (enforced below -- §2 mechanism A)"

# The package names build-iso.sh derives from OMARCHY_ISO_REF (its `case`).
# As of omarchy-iso 174dd82 ("Install the published Omarchy packages in the
# default build") that case maps ONLY edge|dev|local -> the -dev packages, and
# quattro (our ref) -> the released omarchy/omarchy-settings. We pass these in
# explicitly (step 7's `-e`), which overrides the case either way -- named ONCE
# here so the host-side guard 6.4b and the container's package build cannot
# disagree about which files to look for. Same lesson as §3's S1: three
# consumers of one list must read one list.
#
# 🔴 We keep the -dev NAMES on the stable channel, deliberately (the 4.0.0
# stable rebase, 2026-08-15; docs/findings/T9-stable-rebase-remaining.md).
# The released `omarchy` package carries a STATIC pkgver (`4.0.0-1`) with no
# `.g<sha>`, and guard 6.4b's whole job is proving the ISO's runtime was built
# from iso/RUNTIME rather than pulled from the channel -- a check that has no
# purchase on a version that cannot name a commit. `omarchy-dev`'s PKGBUILD
# derives `4.0.0.r<count>.g<sha>` from the source tree's git history, so the
# pin stays verifiable.
#
# This costs nothing in content: measured 2026-08-15, pkgs.omarchy.org/stable
# serves BOTH `omarchy-4.0.0-1` and `omarchy-dev-4.0.0.r1744.gf002044-1` -- and
# that `gf002044` is exactly iso/RUNTIME's pin, i.e. on the stable channel the
# -dev build IS the release commit. (On `edge` it is not: edge already serves
# r1746.gb724f76, two commits past the release. Another reason BUILD_MIRROR is
# stable.)
RUNTIME_PACKAGE=omarchy-dev
SETTINGS_PACKAGE=omarchy-settings-dev
NVIM_PACKAGE=omarchy-nvim

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.1 -- ref and mirror must agree, and this build sets them itself.
#
# As of the stable rebase (2026-08-15, iso/UPSTREAM=174dd82), the known-good
# target is Omarchy 4.0.0 stable, built with **quattro/stable** -- upstream's
# omarchy-iso-make default (OMARCHY_ISO_REF=quattro, OMARCHY_MIRROR=stable) now
# agrees with us, because 174dd82 made the default quattro build install the
# published packages. Refuse any other combination rather than silently
# overriding one a caller already exported -- a caller who set OMARCHY_MIRROR=edge
# expecting the old beta packages must be told no, not built a different ISO than
# they asked for. (Pre-stable this line read quattro/edge; docs/findings/T9-stable-pin.md.)
#
# T5b note: upstream's `omarchy-iso-make --local-source` sets
# OMARCHY_ISO_REF=local. We deliberately keep 'quattro' while mounting the
# same two source trees. At 174dd82 build-iso.sh's `case` maps ONLY edge|dev|local
# -> the -dev packages; quattro and local both fall through to the released
# omarchy/omarchy-settings, so keeping 'quattro' selects the right packages, and
# OMARCHY_ISO_REF's only other effects are the /root/omarchy_iso_ref stamp and
# the debug build-info.
# Switching to 'local' would change that stamp, and with it the one file the
# T5a parity comparison against ~/ISOs/ is measured on
# (docs/findings/T9-iso-comparison.md §2), for no build behaviour at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BUILD_ISO_REF=quattro
BUILD_MIRROR=stable

if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_ISO_REF:-} && $OMARCHY_ISO_REF != "$BUILD_ISO_REF" ]]; then
  build_fail "OMARCHY_ISO_REF=$OMARCHY_ISO_REF is set in the environment, but this fork is pinned to ref '$BUILD_ISO_REF' (guard 6.1, T5-fork-plan.md §6.1). Unset it or bring it into agreement."
fi
if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_MIRROR:-} && $OMARCHY_MIRROR != "$BUILD_MIRROR" ]]; then
  build_fail "OMARCHY_MIRROR=$OMARCHY_MIRROR is set in the environment, but this fork is pinned to mirror '$BUILD_MIRROR' (guard 6.1, T5-fork-plan.md §6.1). Unset it or bring it into agreement."
fi

build_log "guard 6.1 OK: OMARCHY_ISO_REF=$BUILD_ISO_REF OMARCHY_MIRROR=$BUILD_MIRROR (agree)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Scratch locations -- all outside this repo (docs/PROGRESS.md §1.1).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SCRATCH_ROOT=${OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR:-"$HOME/.cache/omarchy-deck/iso-build"}
case $SCRATCH_ROOT in
  "$REPO_ROOT"|"$REPO_ROOT"/*)
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR ($SCRATCH_ROOT) is inside this repo. The ISO and its build cache must never land in git-tracked space -- point it elsewhere." ;;
esac

SCRATCH_SRC="$SCRATCH_ROOT/src"
# Guard 6.3: a SCRATCH pacman cache, never the host's /var/cache/pacman/pkg.
# Persisted across runs deliberately (unlike omarchy-iso-make, which deletes
# and re-mounts the host's every time) -- this is the "same rebuild speed"
# half of the guard's promise.
PACMAN_CACHE_DIR="$SCRATCH_ROOT/pacman-cache"
OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR="$SCRATCH_ROOT/offline-mirror-cache"
RELEASE_DIR="$SCRATCH_ROOT/release"

mkdir -p "$SCRATCH_ROOT" "$PACMAN_CACHE_DIR" "$OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR" "$RELEASE_DIR"
build_log "scratch root: $SCRATCH_ROOT (outside the repo)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2b. The --local-source inputs: the pinned runtime, and the PKGBUILDs.
#
# This is what turns iso/RUNTIME from a note into a pin. Both trees are
# bind-mounted into the container in step 7, where
# builder/build-omarchy-packages.sh builds omarchy-{dev,settings-dev,nvim}
# from them straight into the offline mirror and build-iso.sh drops those
# three from its network download.
#
# ⚠️ build-iso.sh keys two DIFFERENT decisions off these mounts: the local
# package build needs both /omarchy-source and /omarchy-pkgs, but the
# omarchy-base.packages / setup-form.sh sources switch on /omarchy-source
# ALONE. Mount only the first and you get an ISO whose package lists came
# from the pinned checkout while its runtime came from the channel -- the §0
# split, rebuilt by hand. So this mounts both or neither, and "neither" is
# not offered.
#
# 🔴 The third input nobody pinned. --local-source needs the PKGBUILDs too,
# and omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs decides what actually lands in /usr/bin (its
# omarchy-dev recipe ships all of bin/ except three files). T5-fork-plan.md
# §2 names it as a requirement but pins only two of the three inputs, so
# there is no iso/PKGS to enforce and choosing its value is an operator
# decision, not this script's. Until that file exists the path must be given
# explicitly: an unpinned input is tolerable, an unpinned input nobody
# noticed is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# fetch_pinned_checkout <dir> <slug> <sha> <label>
#
# Only ever used for the clone this script MANAGES under the scratch root.
# An operator-supplied checkout is verified and never touched (see
# verify_checkout_at_pin). Re-runnable: an existing clone is reused, and only
# fetched again when it does not already contain the pinned commit.
fetch_pinned_checkout() {
  local dir=$1 slug=$2 sha=$3 label=$4 url="https://github.com/$2.git"
  if [[ ! -e "$dir/.git" ]]; then
    build_log "$label: cloning $slug into $dir (first run; full history -- the PKGBUILDs' pkgver() counts commits)"
    rm -rf "$dir"
    git clone --quiet "$url" "$dir" ||
      build_fail "$label: could not clone $url into $dir"
  fi
  if ! git -C "$dir" cat-file -e "$sha^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
    build_log "$label: $sha not present locally; fetching $slug"
    git -C "$dir" fetch --quiet --tags origin ||
      build_fail "$label: could not fetch $slug into $dir"
  fi
  git -C "$dir" cat-file -e "$sha^{commit}" 2>/dev/null ||
    build_fail "$label: $slug has no commit $sha even after fetching. Is the pin in the right repository?"
  git -C "$dir" checkout --quiet --detach "$sha" ||
    build_fail "$label: could not check $dir out at $sha"
}

if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC:-} ]]; then
  RUNTIME_SRC=$OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC
  [[ -d $RUNTIME_SRC ]] ||
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC=$RUNTIME_SRC is not a directory"
  RUNTIME_SRC=$(cd -- "$RUNTIME_SRC" && pwd)
else
  RUNTIME_SRC="$SCRATCH_ROOT/runtime-src"
  fetch_pinned_checkout "$RUNTIME_SRC" "$RUNTIME_SLUG" "$RUNTIME_PIN" "runtime source"
fi

# Verified the same way whether we checked it out or the caller did -- a
# managed clone that verifies itself by construction would prove nothing.
verify_checkout_at_pin "$RUNTIME_SRC" "$RUNTIME_PIN" "runtime source" \
  "point OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC at a checkout of $RUNTIME_SLUG at $RUNTIME_PIN, unset it to let this script manage one, or update iso/RUNTIME if the pin is meant to move."
RUNTIME_HEAD=$CHECKOUT_HEAD
RUNTIME_SHORT=${RUNTIME_HEAD:0:7}
build_log "runtime pin OK: $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD ($RUNTIME_SRC)"

if [[ -f "$ISO_ROOT/PKGS" ]]; then
  parse_pin "$ISO_ROOT/PKGS"
  PKGS_SLUG=$PIN_SLUG
  PKGS_PIN=$PIN_SHA
  if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC:-} ]]; then
    PKGS_SRC=$OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC
    [[ -d $PKGS_SRC ]] || build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC is not a directory"
    PKGS_SRC=$(cd -- "$PKGS_SRC" && pwd)
  else
    PKGS_SRC="$SCRATCH_ROOT/pkgs-src"
    fetch_pinned_checkout "$PKGS_SRC" "$PKGS_SLUG" "$PKGS_PIN" "pkgs source"
  fi
  verify_checkout_at_pin "$PKGS_SRC" "$PKGS_PIN" "pkgs source" \
    "point OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC at a checkout of $PKGS_SLUG at $PKGS_PIN, unset it to let this script manage one, or update iso/PKGS if the pin is meant to move."
  build_log "pkgs pin OK: $PKGS_SLUG@$CHECKOUT_HEAD ($PKGS_SRC)"
else
  [[ -n ${OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC:-} ]] ||
    build_fail "no iso/PKGS pin file and no OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC. --local-source needs the omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs PKGBUILDs as well as the runtime (T5-fork-plan.md §2 mechanism A), and this repo pins only two of the three build inputs. Clone https://github.com/omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs and set OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=<path>, or create iso/PKGS containing 'omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs@<sha>' to pin it properly -- which is the operator decision this slice deliberately did not make for you."
  PKGS_SRC=$OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC
  [[ -d $PKGS_SRC ]] || build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC is not a directory"
  PKGS_SRC=$(cd -- "$PKGS_SRC" && pwd)
  [[ -e "$PKGS_SRC/.git" ]] ||
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC is not a git checkout. Without a sha there is nothing to record and nothing to reproduce."
  pkgs_dirty=$(git -C "$PKGS_SRC" status --porcelain)
  [[ -z $pkgs_dirty ]] ||
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC has local modifications. The PKGBUILDs decide what lands in /usr/bin; an edited copy makes the result unreproducible and guard 6.4b meaningless:"$'\n'"$pkgs_dirty"
  pkgs_head=$(git -C "$PKGS_SRC" rev-parse HEAD)
  build_log "⚠️  pkgs source is UNPINNED: using $PKGS_SRC at $pkgs_head"
  build_log "⚠️  pin it by creating iso/PKGS with: omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs@$pkgs_head"
fi

# All three, because build-omarchy-packages.sh builds all three and stops on
# the first one it cannot find -- an hour into a container that has already
# provisioned an entire Arch image.
for pkg_name in "$RUNTIME_PACKAGE" "$SETTINGS_PACKAGE" "$NVIM_PACKAGE"; do
  [[ -d "$PKGS_SRC/pkgbuilds/$pkg_name" ]] ||
    build_fail "pkgs source $PKGS_SRC has no pkgbuilds/$pkg_name. build-omarchy-packages.sh would fail inside the container after the whole image had been provisioned; refusing here instead."
done

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Fresh scratch clone of the pinned submodule.
#
# A fresh local clone every run (rather than reusing/resetting one in place)
# is what makes this idempotent and re-runnable (CLAUDE.md) without needing
# to reason about what a previous run's `git apply` or rsync left behind.
# --local hardlinks objects from iso/upstream's own object store, so this is
# cheap; it is not pushed anywhere and never leaves the scratch root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

build_log "cloning pinned upstream into scratch ($SCRATCH_SRC)..."
rm -rf "$SCRATCH_SRC"
git clone --quiet --local --no-hardlinks "$UPSTREAM_DIR" "$SCRATCH_SRC"
git -C "$SCRATCH_SRC" checkout --quiet "$upstream_head"

# archiso is itself a submodule of omarchy-iso (configs/profiledef.sh's
# buildmodes need it; bin/omarchy-iso-make's own first step is
# `git submodule update --init --recursive`). Populate it the same way here.
build_log "populating omarchy-iso's own archiso submodule..."
git -C "$SCRATCH_SRC" submodule update --init --recursive --jobs=8 --quiet ||
  build_fail "could not initialize $SCRATCH_SRC's archiso submodule"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. rsync overlay/ over the scratch copy.
#
# T5a's overlay is deliberately empty (.gitkeep placeholders only, excluded
# below) -- content is a later slice's job (T5-fork-plan.md §7). This step
# still runs unconditionally so that an empty overlay build and a populated
# one go through the exact same code path; that is what makes the parity
# proof in this slice meaningful for the ones after it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

build_log "applying overlay/configs/ ..."
rsync -a --exclude='.gitkeep' "$ISO_ROOT/overlay/configs/" "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. git apply --3way the patches. Fail loudly on any reject.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

shopt -s nullglob
patches=("$ISO_ROOT"/overlay/patches/*.patch)
shopt -u nullglob

if [[ ${#patches[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
  build_log "no patches in overlay/patches/ (expected for T5a's empty overlay)"
else
  for p in "${patches[@]}"; do
    build_log "applying patch: $(basename -- "$p")"
    git -C "$SCRATCH_SRC" apply --3way "$p" ||
      build_fail "patch $(basename -- "$p") did not apply cleanly (git apply --3way left a reject or conflict). Upstream moved under this patch -- do not silently paper over it; rebase the patch by hand."
  done
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5b. Stage the LIVE ISO's on-screen keyboard.
#
# 🔴 THE P1 THIS EXISTS FOR. omarchy-2026.08.15-x86_64-quattro.iso shipped the
# installer's text SCREENS (configs/airootfs/.../deck-form.sh, which the
# overlay rsync above put there) without the BINARY those screens launch to
# draw a keyboard. On the first real-hardware boot the Wi-Fi passphrase prompt
# came up with "mapper not found at /usr/local/bin/deck-input-mapper" and no
# way to type -- on a machine with no keyboard. Same for the username, the
# account password (masked, so a mistake would be invisible) and the hostname:
# all four go through deck_form_text_prompt.
# docs/findings/P32-osk-mapper-missing-from-live-iso.md is the full write-up.
#
# --- WHY A BUILD-TIME COPY OUT OF src/, RATHER THAN AN OVERLAY FILE ---------
#
# The finding named two candidate routes. Neither was the one taken, and the
# reason is worth writing down because "just do it the way deck-form.sh is
# done" is the obvious answer and it is wrong here:
#
#   Route 1, a tracked copy at iso/overlay/configs/airootfs/usr/local/bin/
#   deck-input-mapper. This is how deck-form.sh ships, and for deck-form.sh it
#   is right: that file has no other home, the overlay IS its source. The
#   mapper does have another home -- src/deck-input-mapper.py, 190 KB, under
#   active development, with its own unit suites pointing at src/ and
#   src/deck-session.sh installing it onto the TARGET from there. A second
#   tracked copy of it (plus its two OSK modules, ~80 KB more) would be four
#   files that drift SILENTLY from the ones every test reads. This repo's
#   recurring defect is a second declaration going stale, not a missing one.
#
#   Route 2, carry it in the omarchy-deck package and install that package
#   into the live set. The package is built for the TARGET: it also ships a
#   pacman hook, a patch applier and a systemd unit that have no business
#   running in the live root, and it ships no mapper today. Adding one to it
#   to solve a live-ISO problem couples two unrelated lifetimes.
#
# So: ONE source of truth (src/), copied into the overlaid tree at build time,
# with a guard that reads the CONSUMER's own declaration to decide where it
# goes. The treatment matches `deck-session.sh stage-input-mapper` exactly --
# the script installed 0755 WITHOUT its .py extension, the importable modules
# 0644 in a lib directory beside it -- because the mapper computes its own
# module search path from its install location and the two must agree.
#
# ⚠️ EVERY PATH AND NAME BELOW IS DERIVED, none is written twice:
#   * the destination comes from deck-form.sh's DECK_MAPPER_BIN literal, so
#     moving the path in the consumer moves the file (or fails the build);
#   * the OSK backend comes from deck-form.sh's DECK_MAPPER_ARGS, so asking
#     for a renderer we do not stage fails the build;
#   * the module directory is computed from the destination the same way
#     src/deck-input-mapper.py's OSK_SEARCH_DIRS computes it, and that
#     computation is cross-checked against the mapper's own source below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

LIVE_FORM_SH="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/deck-form.sh"
LIVE_MAPPER_SRC="$REPO_ROOT/src/deck-input-mapper.py"
# The one module the mapper loads unconditionally at import time (it decides
# which keycodes the uinput device DECLARES, and an undeclared keycode is
# dropped by the kernel with no error anywhere). The renderer is derived from
# the backend below and appended to this.
LIVE_OSK_MODULES=(deck_osk_layout.py)

[[ -f $LIVE_FORM_SH ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the live on-screen keyboard: no deck-form.sh at ${LIVE_FORM_SH#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} in the overlaid tree. That file IS the installer's Deck screens; without it there is nothing to stage a keyboard for, and the overlay rsync above should have put it there."

# `|| true` on the pipeline, not on grep: `set -o pipefail` is on, so a grep
# that matches nothing would otherwise abort this script inside the command
# substitution -- silently, with no message, which is the one thing this file
# is not allowed to do (CLAUDE.md). The empty result is handled below, loudly.
LIVE_MAPPER_DEST=$(
  grep -oE '^readonly DECK_MAPPER_BIN=[^[:space:]]+' "$LIVE_FORM_SH" |
    sed -E 's|^readonly DECK_MAPPER_BIN=||' | head -n1 || true
)
[[ -n $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the live on-screen keyboard: no 'readonly DECK_MAPPER_BIN=<path>' in ${LIVE_FORM_SH#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/}. That literal is where deck_form_text_prompt looks for the mapper, and it is the ONLY authority for where this build must put it. Refusing to guess a path -- guessing wrong reproduces the exact P32 failure with a green build log."
[[ $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST == /* ]] ||
  build_fail "deck-form.sh's DECK_MAPPER_BIN is '$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST', which is not an absolute path. It is used as an airootfs destination here and as an executable path on the live ISO; a relative value cannot be either."

LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND=$(
  grep -oE -- '--osk-backend=[A-Za-z0-9_-]+' "$LIVE_FORM_SH" |
    sed -E 's|^--osk-backend=||' | sort -u || true
)
[[ -n $LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the live on-screen keyboard: no --osk-backend=<name> in ${LIVE_FORM_SH#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/}'s DECK_MAPPER_ARGS. The backend decides WHICH renderer module has to be in the image; without it this step would stage a mapper that starts, navigates, and has no keyboard."
[[ $(printf '%s\n' "$LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND" | grep -c .) -eq 1 ]] ||
  build_fail "deck-form.sh names more than one --osk-backend ($(printf '%s' "$LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND" | tr '\n' ' ')). Which renderer the live ISO needs is then ambiguous, and staging the wrong one fails only on hardware."
LIVE_OSK_MODULES+=("deck_osk_${LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND}.py")

# The module directory, computed from the destination exactly as
# src/deck-input-mapper.py's OSK_SEARCH_DIRS computes it: <its own dir>/../lib/
# deck-osk. Cross-checked against the mapper's own source so this cannot drift
# into a directory the mapper will never search -- the failure mode
# test/unit/test-osk-install-layout.sh was written for (present, enumerated,
# silent).
LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST="$(dirname -- "$(dirname -- "$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST")")/lib/deck-osk"
[[ -f $LIVE_MAPPER_SRC ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the live on-screen keyboard: no ${LIVE_MAPPER_SRC#"$REPO_ROOT"/}. That file is the mapper; src/ is its single source of truth and this build copies it rather than keeping a second tracked copy in the overlay."
grep -q '"lib" / "deck-osk"' "$LIVE_MAPPER_SRC" ||
  build_fail "${LIVE_MAPPER_SRC#"$REPO_ROOT"/} no longer computes its OSK search path as <its own dir>/../lib/deck-osk, but this build still stages the modules into $LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST. The mapper would start, navigation would work, and the keyboard would be missing with one line on stderr nobody reads. Move both together."

live_mapper_staged="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST"
install -Dm0755 "$LIVE_MAPPER_SRC" "$live_mapper_staged" ||
  build_fail "could not stage $LIVE_MAPPER_SRC into the airootfs at $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST"
for live_osk_module in "${LIVE_OSK_MODULES[@]}"; do
  [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/src/$live_osk_module" ]] ||
    build_fail "the live ISO's --osk-backend=$LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND needs src/$live_osk_module and there is no such file. Either the backend name in deck-form.sh's DECK_MAPPER_ARGS is wrong, or the module was renamed without it. Refusing to ship a mapper whose keyboard cannot load."
  install -Dm0644 "$REPO_ROOT/src/$live_osk_module" \
    "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs$LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST/$live_osk_module" ||
    build_fail "could not stage src/$live_osk_module into the airootfs at $LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST"
done

# 🔴 AND THE EXECUTABLE BIT, WHICH THE COPY ABOVE DOES NOT SURVIVE.
#
# mkarchiso does `cp -af --no-preserve=ownership,mode` out of the profile's
# airootfs/ (archiso's _make_custom_airootfs). Modes are DISCARDED: everything
# lands 0644 root:root regardless of what it was on the host. The ONLY thing
# that puts the bit back is a matching entry in configs/profiledef.sh's
# `file_permissions` array -- which is why upstream declares one for every one
# of its own /usr/local/bin scripts.
#
# Get this wrong and deck-form.sh's `[[ -x $mapper_bin ]]` is false, the OSK
# never starts, and the operator sees the EXACT P32 message again -- from an
# image that does contain the mapper. That is a worse bug than the one being
# fixed, because the file is right there.
#
# This is done here rather than as a sixth entry in iso/overlay/patches/ for a
# reason beyond the patch budget (T5-fork-plan.md §1 point 3, currently 5/5):
# the array's contents are decided by what THIS step stages, so a hand-written
# patch listing the path would be a second, hand-kept declaration of the first
# -- the staleness this whole block is arranged to avoid. It is derived, and
# the anchor is checked rather than assumed.
LIVE_PROFILEDEF="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/profiledef.sh"
[[ -f $LIVE_PROFILEDEF ]] ||
  build_fail "no configs/profiledef.sh in the overlaid tree. archiso reads the executable bits of everything in airootfs/ from its file_permissions array; without that file the staged mapper ships mode 0644 and deck-form.sh's -x test fails on the ISO."
grep -q '^file_permissions=(' "$LIVE_PROFILEDEF" ||
  build_fail "configs/profiledef.sh has no 'file_permissions=(' array. archiso copies airootfs with --no-preserve=mode, so that array is the only thing that can make $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST executable on the live ISO. Upstream restructured it; find where the modes are declared now and stage the entry there."
if grep -qF "[\"$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST\"]" "$LIVE_PROFILEDEF"; then
  build_log "profiledef.sh already declares $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST; leaving upstream's entry alone"
else
  # Inserted before the array's closing ')' -- the first line that is exactly
  # ')' at or after the declaration. Done in bash rather than with sed/awk so
  # that "the anchor was never found" is a distinct, loud outcome instead of a
  # zero exit over an unchanged file, which is how a stream editor reports
  # matching nothing.
  mapfile -t live_profiledef_lines <"$LIVE_PROFILEDEF"
  live_in_perms=0
  live_perm_inserted=0
  : >"$LIVE_PROFILEDEF.staged"
  for live_line in "${live_profiledef_lines[@]}"; do
    [[ $live_line == 'file_permissions=('* ]] && live_in_perms=1
    if (( live_in_perms )) && [[ $live_line == ')' ]]; then
      printf '  ["%s"]="0:0:0755"\n' "$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST" >>"$LIVE_PROFILEDEF.staged"
      live_in_perms=0
      live_perm_inserted=1
    fi
    printf '%s\n' "$live_line" >>"$LIVE_PROFILEDEF.staged"
  done
  (( live_perm_inserted )) ||
    build_fail "could not declare $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST executable: configs/profiledef.sh opens a file_permissions=( array and never closes it on a line of its own. That is the anchor this insertion needs; refusing to write a profiledef this step could not place an entry in, because the result would be an ISO carrying a mapper it cannot execute."
  mv "$LIVE_PROFILEDEF.staged" "$LIVE_PROFILEDEF"
fi

build_log "staged the live on-screen keyboard: $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST (0755) + ${#LIVE_OSK_MODULES[@]} module(s) in $LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST (${LIVE_OSK_MODULES[*]}), backend '$LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND' as deck-form.sh asks for it"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5c. Stage the SESSION LAYER payload, for configure_deck's `session_bake`.
#
# Different question from 5b, and the two must not be confused. 5b puts the
# on-screen keyboard in the LIVE ISO so the INSTALLER can be typed on. This
# puts src/deck-session.sh and its runtime beside it so the ORCHESTRATOR can
# run it INSIDE THE TARGET, which is what gives the installed Deck its
# session-switching layer -- the "Switch to Desktop" row, the steamos-*
# helpers, and the target-side mapper for Desktop Mode navigation.
#
# Its absence is a measured P1, not a hypothetical: the first hardware install
# produced a Deck whose Steam power menu had no desktop row at all, whose
# steamos-update call ENOENT'd into a user-facing modal, and whose brightness
# slider had no privileged writer (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.32). Every one of those
# capabilities already existed in deck-session.sh; nothing carried it onto the
# ISO, so nothing could run it.
#
# DERIVED, not hand-listed, for the same reason 5b derives its paths: the
# mapper's filename and the OSK module set are declared inside deck-session.sh
# itself, and a second hand-kept copy here goes stale against a rename in
# exactly the silent way this whole file is arranged to prevent.
#
# No profiledef.sh entry is needed here (unlike 5b): nothing in this payload is
# executed from the live ISO. deck_session_bake.stage_payload() copies it into
# the target and chmods it there, so archiso's --no-preserve=mode is harmless.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SESSION_SRC="$REPO_ROOT/src/deck-session.sh"
SESSION_DEST_DIR=/usr/share/omarchy-iso/deck/session
session_staged_dir="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs$SESSION_DEST_DIR"

[[ -f $SESSION_SRC ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: no ${SESSION_SRC#"$REPO_ROOT"/}. That script IS the session layer -- src/ is its single source of truth and configure_deck's session_bake step runs it inside the target. Without it the installed Deck has no way to reach Desktop Mode."

# The mapper's filename, read from deck-session.sh's own constant rather than
# repeated here. Same file, same name, one authority.
SESSION_MAPPER_NAME=$(sed -n 's/^readonly MAPPER_SRC_NAME=\(.*\)$/\1/p' "$SESSION_SRC" | tr -d "\"'" | head -1)
[[ -n $SESSION_MAPPER_NAME ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: no 'readonly MAPPER_SRC_NAME=<file>' in src/deck-session.sh. That constant names the mapper stage-input-mapper installs on the TARGET; refusing to guess it, because guessing wrong ships a session layer whose Desktop Mode navigation is missing and says so only on stderr."

# The OSK module set, likewise read from deck-session.sh's own array. NOTE this
# is the TARGET's set (all backends -- Desktop Mode runs a Wayland compositor),
# which is deliberately WIDER than 5b's live set (tty only). Do not "simplify"
# by sharing one list between them.
session_osk_line=$(grep -m1 '^OSK_MODULES=(' "$SESSION_SRC") ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: no 'OSK_MODULES=(' array in src/deck-session.sh. stage-input-mapper installs those modules on the target; without the list this step would stage a mapper whose keyboard cannot load."
read -r -a SESSION_OSK_MODULES <<<"$(printf '%s' "$session_osk_line" | sed 's/^OSK_MODULES=(//; s/).*$//' | tr -d "\"'")"
(( ${#SESSION_OSK_MODULES[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: src/deck-session.sh's OSK_MODULES=( array is empty. An empty module set is not a valid answer here -- it would ship a target mapper with no keyboard at all."

# 🔴 THE ARRAY IS NOT ALL LITERALS. Its first element is "$OSK_SRC_NAME", a
# variable declared a few lines above it in the same file, so the text scrape
# above yields the seven characters `$OSK_SRC_NAME` -- which is not a filename
# and never will be. Resolve every `$NAME`/`${NAME}` element against a
# `readonly NAME=` in the same file, and refuse anything still unresolved.
#
# Found by reading back what this step actually extracted rather than trusting
# the sed. Left unresolved it would have aborted the build with "there is no
# src/$OSK_SRC_NAME" -- loud, at least, which is the only reason it would not
# have become another silent P32. Do not replace this with a literal list:
# the whole point is that deck-session.sh stays the single authority.
for session_osk_i in "${!SESSION_OSK_MODULES[@]}"; do
  session_osk_raw=${SESSION_OSK_MODULES[$session_osk_i]}
  [[ $session_osk_raw == \$* ]] || continue
  session_osk_var=${session_osk_raw#\$}
  session_osk_var=${session_osk_var#\{}
  session_osk_var=${session_osk_var%\}}
  session_osk_val=$(sed -n "s/^readonly ${session_osk_var}=\(.*\)$/\1/p" "$SESSION_SRC" | tr -d "\"'" | head -1)
  [[ -n $session_osk_val ]] ||
    build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: src/deck-session.sh's OSK_MODULES contains '$session_osk_raw' and this build cannot resolve it -- there is no 'readonly ${session_osk_var}=<file>' in that file. Refusing to stage a module set with an unexpanded variable in it."
  SESSION_OSK_MODULES[session_osk_i]=$session_osk_val
done

install -Dm0644 "$SESSION_SRC" "$session_staged_dir/deck-session.sh" ||
  build_fail "could not stage src/deck-session.sh into the airootfs at $SESSION_DEST_DIR"
[[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/src/$SESSION_MAPPER_NAME" ]] ||
  build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: src/deck-session.sh names $SESSION_MAPPER_NAME as the mapper and there is no src/$SESSION_MAPPER_NAME."
install -Dm0644 "$REPO_ROOT/src/$SESSION_MAPPER_NAME" "$session_staged_dir/$SESSION_MAPPER_NAME" ||
  build_fail "could not stage src/$SESSION_MAPPER_NAME into the airootfs at $SESSION_DEST_DIR"
for session_osk_module in "${SESSION_OSK_MODULES[@]}"; do
  [[ -f "$REPO_ROOT/src/$session_osk_module" ]] ||
    build_fail "cannot stage the session layer: src/deck-session.sh's OSK_MODULES names $session_osk_module and there is no src/$session_osk_module. Either the array is stale or the module was renamed without it."
  install -Dm0644 "$REPO_ROOT/src/$session_osk_module" "$session_staged_dir/$session_osk_module" ||
    build_fail "could not stage src/$session_osk_module into the airootfs at $SESSION_DEST_DIR"
done

build_log "staged the session layer: $SESSION_DEST_DIR/{deck-session.sh,$SESSION_MAPPER_NAME} + ${#SESSION_OSK_MODULES[@]} OSK module(s) (${SESSION_OSK_MODULES[*]}), for configure_deck's session_bake step"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. The guards. 6.1 already ran above (it gates which env vars reach Docker
#    at all, so it has to run before this point). 6.3 is structural -- it is
#    the scratch PACMAN_CACHE_DIR set up in step 2 and the absence, anywhere
#    in this script, of `sudo rm -rf` against a host path. Recorded here so
#    a reader looking for "guard 6.3" finds this comment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

build_log "guard 6.3 OK: pacman cache is scratch ($PACMAN_CACHE_DIR), host cache untouched"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.4a -- the runtime/finalizer coupling, checked before Docker starts.
#
# T5-fork-plan.md §6.4: "assert that every /usr/bin/omarchy-* the orchestrator
# shells out to actually exists in [the runtime]. Derive the list by grepping
# our patched phases_impl.py ... so the guard cannot go stale against our own
# edits."
#
# Scope is the whole orchestrator directory, not phases_impl.py alone: §3's S3
# puts our configure_deck phase in a NEW module beside it, and a guard that
# only ever reads the file that exists today would go blind to exactly the
# code we are about to add.
#
# Scope stops there on purpose, though. configs/airootfs/usr/local/bin/
# omarchy-install-dashboard also names /usr/bin/omarchy-upload-log, but it
# probes four candidate paths and carries on without any of them -- treating
# a deliberately optional lookup as a hard requirement is the false positive
# that teaches people to ignore a check (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17). The
# orchestrator's calls are subprocess.run(..., check=True) into arch-chroot;
# those are the ones that abort an install two-thirds of the way through.
#
# What this half CANNOT prove: that the package built from this checkout ships
# the file. omarchy-dev's PKGBUILD excludes three names from bin/ (they come
# from omarchy-settings-dev instead), so bin/ is an over-approximation of
# /usr/bin. Guard 6.4b closes that against the real artifact after the build.
# Both are needed; this one is here because it costs a grep and saves ~6 GB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ORCHESTRATOR_DIR="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/orchestrator"
[[ -d $ORCHESTRATOR_DIR ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: no orchestrator at ${ORCHESTRATOR_DIR#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} in the overlaid tree. Upstream moved it, or the overlay clobbered it. Refusing to pass a guard that cannot see its subject."

# Trailing '.' stripped: a name at the end of a sentence in a comment or log
# string would otherwise be reported as a missing binary called 'omarchy-foo.'.
mapfile -t REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS < <(
  grep -rhoE '/usr/bin/omarchy-[A-Za-z0-9._-]+' "$ORCHESTRATOR_DIR" |
    sed -E 's|^/usr/bin/||; s/\.+$//' | sort -u
)
(( ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a found no /usr/bin/omarchy-* references in the orchestrator at all. The orchestrator does call the runtime (run_system_finalizer, run_chroot_finalizer), so zero matches means the pattern went stale, not that the coupling went away. A guard with an empty subject is not a passing guard."

[[ -d "$RUNTIME_SRC/bin" ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: the runtime checkout $RUNTIME_SRC has no bin/ directory."
mapfile -t RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES < <(
  find "$RUNTIME_SRC/bin" -maxdepth 1 \( -type f -o -type l \) -name 'omarchy-*' -printf '%f\n' | sort -u
)
(( ${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: $RUNTIME_SRC/bin contains no omarchy-* files. That is not a runtime checkout."

# --- the SECOND provider set: binaries our OWN package ships ---------------
#
# 6.4a's original premise was "every /usr/bin/omarchy-* the orchestrator calls
# comes from the pinned basecamp/omarchy checkout". That premise broke the
# moment our own configure_deck phase started shelling out to
# /usr/bin/omarchy-deck-apply-patches (T12): that binary comes from the
# omarchy-deck package in THIS repo, and the pinned runtime will never ship it.
# Against one provider the guard fails with a completely wrong diagnosis --
# "the pins have drifted" sends the reader off to compare two commits that are
# both fine.
#
# It was dodged rather than fixed for a day: deck_patches.py composes the path
# from APPLIER_DIR + APPLIER_NAME so the grep above cannot see it, kept honest
# by test/unit/test-deck-configure-patches.py running this guard's own regex
# over that file. That works and it is fragile -- the next person writes the
# literal and gets the wrong diagnosis. This is the fix; the dodge can go.
#
# DERIVED, never hand-kept, for exactly the reason the name list above is
# derived (§6.4): an exemption list maintained by hand goes stale against our
# own next edit, and it goes stale silently -- which is worse than not having
# one, because it also buys false confidence. The authority for "what lands in
# /usr/bin" is the package() body of our PKGBUILD, so that is what is read --
# the same question 6.4b asks of the real .pkg.tar.zst, asked here of the
# recipe, before Docker.
#
# Read from the OVERLAID tree, not from iso/overlay/, for the same reason the
# orchestrator is: this is the copy build-iso.sh will be handed.
DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/deck/pkgbuilds/omarchy-deck"
DECK_PKGBUILD="$DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR/PKGBUILD"
DECK_PROVIDED_BINS=()
DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE=""

if [[ -f $DECK_PKGBUILD ]]; then
  mapfile -t DECK_PROVIDED_BINS < <(
    grep -oE '[$][{]?pkgdir[}]?/usr/bin/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+' "$DECK_PKGBUILD" |
      sed -E 's|.*/usr/bin/||' | sort -u
  )
  if (( ${#DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} > 0 )); then
    DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE="omarchy-deck's PKGBUILD installs ${#DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} into /usr/bin: ${DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[*]}"
  elif grep -qE '[$][{]?pkgdir[}]?/usr/bin' "$DECK_PKGBUILD"; then
    # It writes into /usr/bin and this derivation could not name one thing it
    # puts there -- `install -t "$pkgdir/usr/bin"`, or an `install -d` plus a
    # cp, both of which name their targets somewhere this expression cannot
    # see. Under-deriving is the one outcome that must never be quiet: it
    # would turn into "our package does not ship this either" against a
    # package that ships it perfectly well.
    build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: $DECK_PKGBUILD installs into \$pkgdir/usr/bin, but this guard could not derive a single binary NAME from it.

  The derivation looks for an explicit '\$pkgdir/usr/bin/<name>' target, which
  is the form docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §5 specifies and the form
  test/t12-patch-seam-container-e2e.sh proved. A target-directory install
  ('install -t \"\$pkgdir/usr/bin\" ...') names its files somewhere this
  expression cannot see them.

  Name the targets explicitly in package(), or teach this derivation the other
  form -- but do not let it silently resolve to the empty set, because then
  every binary our own package ships would be reported as shipped by nobody."
  else
    DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE="omarchy-deck's PKGBUILD installs nothing into /usr/bin"
  fi
else
  DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE="there is no omarchy-deck PKGBUILD at ${DECK_PKGBUILD#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} in the overlaid tree, so our package provides nothing"
fi

# WHICH PACKAGES OF OURS THIS BUILD OWES, derived here and consumed by 6.4b.
#
# The patched builder/build-iso.sh (iso/overlay/patches/deck-packages.patch,
# seam S4) declares the set exactly once, as "every directory under
# configs/deck/pkgbuilds/ that holds a PKGBUILD", and builds each one straight
# into the offline mirror. Deriving the same set from the same directory -- in
# the OVERLAID tree, which is the copy the container was handed -- is what lets
# 6.4b say "this package should be in the mirror and is not" instead of
# discovering an absence it has no expectation to compare against.
#
# Read here rather than in 6.4b's own block only because the scratch tree is
# what it is at this point and nothing between here and Docker touches it; the
# consumer is 300 lines down.
DECK_PKGBUILDS_ROOT="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/deck/pkgbuilds"
DECK_PACKAGE_NAMES=()
if [[ -d $DECK_PKGBUILDS_ROOT ]]; then
  while IFS= read -r deck_pkg_dir; do
    [[ -f "$deck_pkg_dir/PKGBUILD" ]] || continue
    DECK_PACKAGE_NAMES+=("$(basename -- "$deck_pkg_dir")")
  done < <(find "$DECK_PKGBUILDS_ROOT" -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -type d | sort)
fi

# The union of the two provider sets. It is a union, not a bypass: a name that
# neither ships is still a hard failure, and the message below has to say WHICH
# provider was supposed to have it, because they are different bugs with
# different fixes.
missing_bins=()
deck_supplied_bins=()
for want in "${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]}"; do
  found=0
  for have in "${RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]}"; do
    [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; break; }
  done
  if (( ! found )); then
    for have in "${DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[@]}"; do
      [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; deck_supplied_bins+=("$want"); break; }
    done
  fi
  (( found )) || missing_bins+=("$want")
done

if (( ${#missing_bins[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_fail "guard 6.4a: the orchestrator calls binaries that NO provider ships.

  installer     $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head (iso/UPSTREAM)
  runtime       $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD (iso/RUNTIME, $RUNTIME_SRC)
  our package   $DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE

  Shelled out to, shipped by neither:
$(printf '    /usr/bin/%s\n' "${missing_bins[@]}")
  Two different bugs wear this shape, and the fix is not the same one:

    * THE PINNED RUNTIME DOES NOT SHIP IT. That is T5-fork-plan.md §0: the git
      ref and the package channel are two independent pins and they have
      drifted apart -- a rename or a removal between them, not an empty
      checkout (the runtime offers ${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} omarchy-* binaries in bin/). Move both
      pins together, or neither.

    * OUR PACKAGE DOES NOT SHIP IT EITHER. If the name is one of ours, nothing
      is wrong with the pins at all: the omarchy-deck PKGBUILD's package() body
      does not install it, so add it there. Its current /usr/bin list is on the
      'our package' line above.

  Left alone, either one fails at 'Configuring system', phase 5 of 14, AFTER
  partitioning, LUKS, pacstrap and ~1200 packages."
fi

if (( ${#deck_supplied_bins[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_log "guard 6.4a OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries (${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[*]}) are shipped by a provider -- ${#deck_supplied_bins[@]} by our own omarchy-deck package (${deck_supplied_bins[*]}), the rest by $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT's bin/ (${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} candidates)"
else
  build_log "guard 6.4a OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries (${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[*]}) exist in $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT's bin/ (${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} candidates); $DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.6 -- every runtime patch still applies to the pinned runtime.
#
# docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §1 point 4: "`git apply --check` every
# patch in src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches/ against the pinned
# basecamp/omarchy checkout, fail on a reject, name the patch and both SHAs."
#
# WHY THIS IS A BUILD GUARD AND NOT A TEST. These patches are not applied here.
# They are applied on the TARGET, at pacman time, by
# omarchy-deck-apply-patches, weeks after this ISO was written. So a patch that
# upstream has moved out from under does not break anything visible: the applier
# reports a reject into /var/lib/omarchy-deck/patch-state.json, and unless
# somebody reads it the Deck simply, quietly, loses the customisation -- the
# lock screen goes back to blanking the panel in ~2 s instead of ~20 s
# (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.24). docs/findings/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §4 is the
# whole argument for this guard in one line: FAIL AT BUILD, NOT AT INSTALL.
# This is the cheapest possible place to discover "the RUNTIME pin moved and
# our patch is stale" -- before an ISO exists at all.
#
# 🔴 --check AND NOTHING ELSE. Not --3way, not fuzz, not a sed fallback. The
# reject IS the product of this seam: the patches carry context lines
# deliberately (the lock timer's literal was retuned three times in two months,
# and a sed would have matched nothing, exited 0, and lost the fix in silence
# -- 0010's .meta says so). Anything that makes a stale patch "apply anyway" is
# the failure mode this mechanism exists to make loud.
#
# Nor --reverse --check as an "already applied, fine" escape hatch:
# verify_checkout_at_pin above refuses a dirty runtime checkout, so "already
# applied" is impossible here by construction, and treating it as a pass would
# mean silently accepting a mutated checkout.
#
# Placed here -- after 6.4a, the last consumer of $RUNTIME_SRC, and before
# Docker -- for 6.4a's reason: it costs a git apply --check per patch and it
# refuses the build before six gigabytes of downloads.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# WHERE THE PATCHES LIVE. Searched, not hard-coded. They ship INSIDE the
# omarchy-deck package (docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §5: "the patches
# ship in the package, so the package payload is one directory"), and that
# directory was still moving out of src/ and into the package's pkgbuild dir
# while this was being written.
#
# Candidates in preference order. The package's own pkgbuild directory comes
# first, and the directory ITSELF before any subdirectory of it, because
# makepkg's source=() cannot reach outside that directory -- so the payload is
# flat beside the PKGBUILD, which is exactly where a `find -name patches` would
# NOT have looked. Subdirectories are swept too, in case it is ever nested. The
# repo-side src/ home is the last resort, and when both hold patches the run
# says so rather than picking one quietly.
runtime_patch_dirs=()
if [[ -d $DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR ]]; then
  runtime_patch_dirs+=("$DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR")
  while IFS= read -r dir; do
    runtime_patch_dirs+=("$dir")
  done < <(find "$DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR" -mindepth 1 -type d | sort)
fi
if [[ -d "$REPO_ROOT/src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches" ]]; then
  runtime_patch_dirs+=("$REPO_ROOT/src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches")
fi

# A guard with no subject is not a passing guard -- 6.4a's empty-match case,
# inherited. "No patches anywhere" does not mean the seam went away; it means
# the payload went missing, and a silent pass would ship a Deck that quietly
# lost every customisation the seam exists to keep.
(( ${#runtime_patch_dirs[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.6 cannot run: no runtime-patch directory found.

  looked in
    ${DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} and below   (inside the omarchy-deck package, as shipped)
    src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches                 (the repo-side home)

  These patches are the T12 upstream-patch seam (docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md).
  Their absence is not 'nothing to check' -- it is the payload having gone
  missing, which on the Deck looks like the lock screen blanking the panel in
  ~2 s again with nothing anywhere saying why. If the seam was retired on
  purpose, remove this guard on purpose."

RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR=""
runtime_patches=()
runtime_patch_dupes=()
for dir in "${runtime_patch_dirs[@]}"; do
  shopt -s nullglob
  dir_patches=("$dir"/*.patch)
  shopt -u nullglob
  (( ${#dir_patches[@]} > 0 )) || continue
  if [[ -z $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR ]]; then
    RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR=$dir
    runtime_patches=("${dir_patches[@]}")
  else
    runtime_patch_dupes+=("$dir")
  fi
done

[[ -n $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.6 cannot run: a runtime-patch directory exists but holds no *.patch files.

  looked in
$(printf '    %s\n' "${runtime_patch_dirs[@]}")
  An empty patch directory is the same failure as a missing one, and it is the
  likelier of the two: a move that took the directory and left the files, or a
  payload copy step that globbed the wrong thing. Refusing to report a pass
  over an empty set (docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §5.4)."

if (( ${#runtime_patch_dupes[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_log "guard 6.6: checking $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR (the copy the omarchy-deck package ships). Also present: ${runtime_patch_dupes[*]} -- two copies of one patch set is a drift waiting to happen, and only the shipped one is checked here."
fi

# Every failure is collected, not the first one: a RUNTIME pin that moves
# usually breaks more than one patch at once, and rebasing them one build at a
# time is three 40-minute round trips to learn what one run already knew.
patch_rejects=()
for p in "${runtime_patches[@]}"; do
  patch_name=$(basename -- "$p")
  if reject_detail=$(git -C "$RUNTIME_SRC" apply --check -p1 -- "$p" 2>&1); then
    continue
  fi
  # The user-visible consequence comes from the patch's own .meta
  # `requirement:` line -- derived, so it cannot describe a patch this repo no
  # longer carries, and so a new patch gets a real consequence in this message
  # without anybody remembering to add one here.
  consequence=""
  if [[ -f "${p%.patch}.meta" ]]; then
    consequence=$(sed -nE 's/^requirement:[[:space:]]*//p' "${p%.patch}.meta" | head -n1)
  fi
  [[ -n $consequence ]] ||
    consequence="(no 'requirement:' line in ${patch_name%.patch}.meta, so this patch does not say what it is for -- fix that too)"
  patch_rejects+=("    $patch_name
      what it is for: $consequence
      git apply --check said:
$(printf '%s\n' "$reject_detail" | sed 's/^/        /')")
done

if (( ${#patch_rejects[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_fail "guard 6.6: ${#patch_rejects[@]} of ${#runtime_patches[@]} runtime patch(es) no longer apply to the pinned runtime.

  installer   $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head (iso/UPSTREAM)
  runtime     $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD (iso/RUNTIME, $RUNTIME_SRC)
  patches     $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR

$(printf '%s\n' "${patch_rejects[@]}")
  Upstream moved under these patches. Nothing here is broken yet -- which is
  exactly why this must stop the build: the patches are applied on the TARGET at
  pacman time, so a stale one costs you nothing today and silently drops the
  customisation on every Deck this ISO installs. That is the trade
  docs/findings/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §4 names: fail at BUILD, not at
  install.

  🔴 Rebase the patch against $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT by hand, and update
  its .meta post-conditions in the same commit. Do NOT reach for --3way, fuzz,
  or a sed fallback to make it 'apply anyway': the reject is the PRODUCT of this
  seam, not an obstacle to it. If upstream made the change deliberately and the
  patch is now pointless, retire the patch and its .meta together."
fi

build_log "guard 6.6 OK: all ${#runtime_patches[@]} runtime patch(es) in ${RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR#"$REPO_ROOT"/} still apply cleanly to $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT ($(printf '%s ' "${runtime_patches[@]##*/}"))"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.7 -- the live ISO carries the keyboard its own screens require.
#
# docs/findings/P32-osk-mapper-missing-from-live-iso.md, point 3 of its fix
# outline: "the whole point of the guard family is to catch exactly this 'the
# screen references a thing the image does not carry' gap."
#
# 6.4a asks that question of the TARGET (does the runtime ship the binaries the
# orchestrator shells out to). Nothing asked it of the LIVE ROOT, and that is
# how an ISO shipped whose installer screens launch /usr/local/bin/
# deck-input-mapper on an image that contained no such file, no OSK modules and
# no python-evdev. Every tier below hardware was structurally blind to it: the
# unit suites drive deck-form.sh's functions directly against fake mappers, and
# the QEMU harness never presses a button at the live tty.
#
# FOUR ASSERTIONS, and they are deliberately asked of DIFFERENT ARTIFACTS --
# three of them about things step 5b just did, and the fourth about something
# no step here controls:
#
#   a. the mapper is in the overlaid airootfs, at the path deck-form.sh's own
#      DECK_MAPPER_BIN names, non-empty and executable ON THE HOST SIDE;
#   b. configs/profiledef.sh declares that path with an executable mode, so it
#      is still executable after mkarchiso's --no-preserve=mode copy. (a)
#      without (b) is the same visible failure from a different cause;
#   c. every OSK module the declared backend needs is in the directory the
#      mapper will search;
#   d. the live package list carries python-evdev, AND the patched
#      builder/build-iso.sh actually reads that list into packages.x86_64. The
#      second half is the one that matters: a list nothing consumes is a
#      comment, and it would leave the mapper dying at its first import line
#      with the screens still launching it.
#
# Checking (a)-(c) against work this script did four hundred lines up is not
# circular busywork: what it pins is the COUPLING to files this script does not
# own -- deck-form.sh's path and backend, src/'s module names, upstream's
# profiledef structure. Any of those moving is what would break, and each one
# fails here with a diagnosis naming the file to fix rather than with a Wi-Fi
# screen nobody can type into.
#
# The names are derived (§6.4's rule): the dependency below comes from what the
# staged Python actually imports, not from a hand-kept list that would go stale
# against our own next edit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# (a) the mapper itself.
[[ -f $live_mapper_staged ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: nothing at airootfs$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST in the overlaid tree, but ${LIVE_FORM_SH#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} launches exactly that path to draw the installer's on-screen keyboard. Step 5b should have staged it. This is the P32 shape: screens in the image, keyboard not."
[[ -s $live_mapper_staged ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: airootfs$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST is EMPTY. A zero-byte mapper passes an -x test, starts, exits immediately, and deck-form.sh degrades on a timeout instead of on a missing file -- the same screen, a worse diagnosis."
[[ -x $live_mapper_staged ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: airootfs$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST is not executable in the source tree. deck-form.sh gates on '[[ -x \$mapper_bin ]]' and would print 'mapper not found' about a file that is right there."

# (b) and the bit survives mkarchiso.
live_perm_entry=$(grep -F "[\"$LIVE_MAPPER_DEST\"]" "$LIVE_PROFILEDEF" || true)
[[ -n $live_perm_entry ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: configs/profiledef.sh declares no file_permissions entry for $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST.

  archiso copies the profile's airootfs/ with 'cp -af --no-preserve=ownership,mode'
  (_make_custom_airootfs). The host-side mode is DISCARDED -- the file lands 0644
  root:root -- and file_permissions is the only thing that puts the executable bit
  back. Upstream declares one for each of its own /usr/local/bin/omarchy-* scripts
  for this reason.

  Without it the ISO contains the mapper and cannot run it, and deck-form.sh
  reports 'mapper not found' -- which sends the next reader looking for a missing
  file instead of a missing mode."
# The entry's shape is ["<path>"]="<uid>:<gid>:<mode>". archiso splits it on ':'
# and hands the third field straight to chmod, so the OWNER digit of that field
# -- third from the right, because the mode may or may not carry a leading
# setuid digit -- is literally the bit deck-form.sh's -x test will see (the
# installer runs as root). Checking the LAST digit instead would pass a 0700
# and fail an 0711, both of which are wrong answers.
live_perm_mode=${live_perm_entry##*:}
live_perm_mode=${live_perm_mode%%\"*}
[[ $live_perm_mode =~ ^[0-7]{3,4}$ ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: cannot read a mode out of configs/profiledef.sh's entry for $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST:
    $live_perm_entry
  Expected [\"<path>\"]=\"<uid>:<gid>:<mode>\". Refusing to assume a mode this guard could not parse -- an unreadable declaration is not a passing one."
case ${live_perm_mode: -3:1} in
  1|3|5|7) ;;
  *) build_fail "guard 6.7: configs/profiledef.sh declares $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST mode $live_perm_mode, which does not grant execute to its owner. archiso applies this verbatim with chmod after copying, so this IS the mode on the shipped ISO, and deck-form.sh's '[[ -x \$mapper_bin ]]' would be false against a file that is present." ;;
esac

# (c) the modules the declared backend needs, where the mapper will look.
live_missing_modules=()
for live_osk_module in "${LIVE_OSK_MODULES[@]}"; do
  [[ -s "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs$LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST/$live_osk_module" ]] ||
    live_missing_modules+=("$live_osk_module")
done
(( ${#live_missing_modules[@]} == 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: the live ISO asks for --osk-backend=$LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND but airootfs$LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST is missing ${live_missing_modules[*]}. The mapper resolves that directory from its own install path, finds nothing, prints one line on stderr and CARRIES ON -- navigation works, there are no character keys, and the install looks like it merely timed out. Stage the modules with the script, as a set."

# (d) the runtime dependency, derived from what the staged Python imports, and
#     proved to be reachable by the thing that installs it.
#
# The module -> package mapping is the one hand-written fact here, and it is
# small on purpose: everything else about this check (which files to scan, and
# whether they import at all) comes from the staged tree.
declare -A LIVE_PKG_FOR_PYTHON_MODULE=(
  [evdev]=python-evdev
)

LIVE_PACKAGES_LIST="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/deck/deck-live.packages"
[[ -f $LIVE_PACKAGES_LIST ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: no configs/deck/deck-live.packages in the overlaid tree. That file is the LIVE root's package list -- the target's list (deck-install.packages) is a different question and does not put anything in the ISO you boot."

mapfile -t LIVE_PACKAGE_NAMES < <(grep -hv '^#\|^$' "$LIVE_PACKAGES_LIST" | tr -d '[:blank:]' | sort -u)
(( ${#LIVE_PACKAGE_NAMES[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: configs/deck/deck-live.packages holds no package names (empty, or entirely comments). A guard with an empty subject is not a passing guard."

# Which third-party Python modules the staged OSK payload imports at module
# scope. Restricted to the names we can map to a package, because an unmapped
# import is a question for a human, not a silent pass -- see the loop below.
mapfile -t LIVE_PY_IMPORTS < <(
  grep -hoE '^(from|import) [a-z_][a-z0-9_]*' \
    "$live_mapper_staged" \
    "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs$LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST"/*.py |
    sed -E 's/^(from|import) //' | sort -u
)
(( ${#LIVE_PY_IMPORTS[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7 cannot run: no top-level imports found in the staged mapper or its OSK modules. The mapper imports evdev at module scope, so zero matches means this derivation went stale, not that the dependency went away."

live_unsatisfied_deps=()
for live_py_module in "${LIVE_PY_IMPORTS[@]}"; do
  live_dep_pkg=${LIVE_PKG_FOR_PYTHON_MODULE[$live_py_module]:-}
  [[ -n $live_dep_pkg ]] || continue          # stdlib, or a sibling OSK module
  live_dep_found=0
  for live_pkg in "${LIVE_PACKAGE_NAMES[@]}"; do
    [[ $live_pkg == "$live_dep_pkg" ]] && { live_dep_found=1; break; }
  done
  (( live_dep_found )) || live_unsatisfied_deps+=("$live_py_module -> $live_dep_pkg")
done
(( ${#live_unsatisfied_deps[@]} == 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: the staged on-screen keyboard imports modules the live ISO does not install.

$(printf '    %s\n' "${live_unsatisfied_deps[@]}")
  configs/deck/deck-live.packages currently carries: ${LIVE_PACKAGE_NAMES[*]}

  An ImportError at module scope kills the mapper before it can print its
  'deck-input-mapper: bound' marker, so deck-form.sh waits out its 5 s deadline
  and degrades -- with the mapper file present and executable, which makes the
  message it prints actively misleading. Add the package to that list."

# ...and the list has to be READ by something. A package list nothing consumes
# is a comment: it would leave every assertion above satisfied and the ISO
# still missing the dependency.
LIVE_PATCHED_BUILDER="$SCRATCH_SRC/builder/build-iso.sh"
[[ -f $LIVE_PATCHED_BUILDER ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7 cannot run: no builder/build-iso.sh in the overlaid tree to check the live package list against."
grep -q 'deck-live\.packages' "$LIVE_PATCHED_BUILDER" ||
  build_fail "guard 6.7: nothing in the patched builder/build-iso.sh reads configs/deck/deck-live.packages, so its entries reach neither packages.x86_64 nor the offline mirror. The list exists, the guard above is satisfied by it, and the ISO carries none of it -- a check passing for the wrong reason. iso/overlay/patches/deck-packages.patch (seam S6) is what appends it; it has probably been dropped or rebased away."

build_log "guard 6.7 OK: the live root carries $LIVE_MAPPER_DEST (executable, and declared 0755 in profiledef.sh so mkarchiso's --no-preserve=mode copy cannot strip it), ${#LIVE_OSK_MODULES[@]} OSK module(s) for backend '$LIVE_MAPPER_BACKEND' in $LIVE_OSK_LIB_DEST, and ${#LIVE_PACKAGE_NAMES[@]} live package(s) build-iso.sh installs (${LIVE_PACKAGE_NAMES[*]})"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.8 -- a package list nothing installs from is a comment.
#
# docs/findings/P32-steam-never-installed.md, point 3 of its fix outline: "a
# guard that a list with no consumer is an error. The general defect is that a
# package list can exist, be maintained, be unit-tested, and install nothing."
#
# That is not hypothetical. `steam` and `steamdeck-dsp` sat in
# configs/deck/deck-fetch.packages, correctly spelled, documented, and covered
# by a unit suite. Nothing installed them. The ISO shipped, the installer's own
# record reported ELEVEN GREEN STEPS, the QEMU install harness scored 18/18,
# and the Deck booted to a black panel because /usr/lib/steam did not exist.
# The only thing in the whole repo that ever opened that file was
# configs/deck/deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh, which reads it to ask the NVIDIA
# question (docs/PROGRESS.md §3.8) and installs precisely nothing.
#
# Guard 6.7 (d) asks this of ONE list -- deck-live.packages, against the
# patched builder. This asks it of EVERY list in configs/deck/, DERIVED from
# the directory listing rather than from three names written here, because "a
# fourth list nobody remembered to wire up" is the shape of the bug itself.
#
# --- WHAT COUNTS AS A CONSUMER, AND WHY THE SCOPE IS DRAWN HERE ------------
#
# In scope -- the two places code that actually installs can live:
#
#   builder/           what runs inside the container: mkarchiso's live
#                      pacstrap (packages.x86_64), the offline mirror's
#                      `pacman -Syw` download list, and the merge into the
#                      shipped omarchy-base.packages that the TARGET's own
#                      pacstrap and the dashboard's denominator both read.
#   configs/airootfs/  what the live ISO carries and runs -- the install
#                      orchestrator (usr/share/omarchy-iso/orchestrator/)
#                      included. A list installed AT INSTALL TIME, over the
#                      network, is consumed from here.
#
# Out of scope, and each exclusion is the entire point of this guard:
#
#   configs/deck/ itself. That is where the lists live -- and where their
#     pre-flight CHECKER lives. deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh reads
#     deck-fetch.packages; reading a list to ask a question ABOUT it is not
#     installing it. Counting that read would have made this guard green on
#     the exact tree that shipped a Deck with no Steam.
#   this script. iso/bin/build reads deck-live.packages forty lines up -- as a
#     CHECK. A guard that accepts its own read as proof of a consumer is
#     circular, and would pass the same shape it exists to catch.
#
# ⚠️ THE LIMITATION, STATED RATHER THAN PAPERED OVER. Inside those two
# directories this guard CANNOT tell an installer from a checker: it matches
# the file's NAME, not what is done with it. A checker moved into builder/
# would satisfy it. What the scope does buy is that a checker has to live
# where the installing code lives -- a far smaller hiding place than "anywhere
# in the repo" -- and the failure below NAMES every out-of-scope reader it
# found, so nobody has to guess what was discounted or why.
#
# Matches inside comments do not count, either. The patched build-iso.sh
# carries a three-line comment naming the other three lists side by side; a
# list mentioned only in that prose would otherwise pass on the strength of
# being documented, which is exactly how deck-fetch.packages felt safe.
#
# ⚠️ "Comment" here means a line whose first non-blank character is '#' --
# shell and Python line comments, which is what both consumer directories are
# written in. A list named ONLY inside a Python docstring (no '#', and the
# orchestrator modules open with long ones) would still count as a reference.
# Narrowing that would mean parsing Python from bash; the honest trade is to
# say so here rather than to imply a precision this does not have.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DECK_LIST_DIR="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/deck"
[[ -d $DECK_LIST_DIR ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.8 cannot run: no configs/deck/ in the overlaid tree. That directory holds every Deck package list; without it this guard has no subject, and a guard with no subject is not a passing guard. The overlay rsync in step 4 should have put it there."

mapfile -t DECK_PACKAGE_LISTS < <(
  find "$DECK_LIST_DIR" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.packages' -printf '%f\n' | LC_ALL=C sort
)
(( ${#DECK_PACKAGE_LISTS[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.8 cannot run: configs/deck/ holds no *.packages files at all. This build installs Deck packages from those lists, so zero of them means the overlay is wrong or this derivation went stale -- not that there is nothing to check."

DECK_CONSUMER_DIRS=("$SCRATCH_SRC/builder" "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs")
for deck_consumer_dir in "${DECK_CONSUMER_DIRS[@]}"; do
  [[ -d $deck_consumer_dir ]] ||
    build_fail "guard 6.8 cannot run: no ${deck_consumer_dir#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/}/ in the overlaid tree. That is one of the two places a package list's consumer can live, and searching only the other one would report a missing consumer as a missing directory's fault -- or, worse, report a pass. Upstream moved it, or the overlay clobbered it."
done

# deck_list_readers <filename> <dir>...
# Prints "<path-relative-to-the-tree>:<line>" for every NON-COMMENT line under
# the given directories that names <filename>. A line whose first non-blank
# character is '#' is prose about a list, not a use of one -- see the comment
# block above for the specific three-line comment that makes this necessary.
#
# Split in bash rather than with awk on purpose: `awk` on Arch is a SYMLINK to
# gawk, and test/unit/test-iso-build.sh builds its no-docker PATH out of
# `find -type f`, which does not follow symlinks. An awk in here parses
# perfectly, shellchecks clean, and silently produces nothing under the unit
# suite -- a guard that passes for the wrong reason, in the guard whose whole
# subject is checks that pass for the wrong reason.
deck_list_readers() {
  local needle=$1
  shift
  local dir hit path rest lineno text
  for dir in "$@"; do
    [[ -d $dir ]] || continue
    while IFS= read -r hit; do
      path=${hit%%:*}
      rest=${hit#*:}
      lineno=${rest%%:*}
      text=${rest#*:}
      text=${text#"${text%%[![:space:]]*}"}
      if [[ $text == '#'* ]]; then
        continue
      fi
      printf '%s:%s\n' "${path#"$SCRATCH_SRC/"}" "$lineno"
    done < <(grep -rnF --binary-files=without-match -- "$needle" "$dir" 2>/dev/null || true)
  done
}

deck_orphan_lists=()
deck_consumer_report=()
for deck_list in "${DECK_PACKAGE_LISTS[@]}"; do
  mapfile -t deck_readers < <(deck_list_readers "$deck_list" "${DECK_CONSUMER_DIRS[@]}")
  if (( ${#deck_readers[@]} == 0 )); then
    deck_orphan_lists+=("$deck_list")
  else
    deck_consumer_report+=("$deck_list <- ${deck_readers[0]} (${#deck_readers[@]} non-comment reference(s))")
  fi
done

if (( ${#deck_orphan_lists[@]} > 0 )); then
  # For the diagnosis only: who DOES read these, outside the consumer scope.
  # Handing the reader that list is the difference between "wire it up" and an
  # hour spent arguing with a guard about a file they know something opens.
  deck_orphan_detail=()
  for deck_list in "${deck_orphan_lists[@]}"; do
    mapfile -t deck_elsewhere < <(
      deck_list_readers "$deck_list" "$DECK_LIST_DIR" | grep -v "^configs/deck/$deck_list:" || true
    )
    if (( ${#deck_elsewhere[@]} > 0 )); then
      deck_orphan_detail+=("$deck_list -- read ONLY by: ${deck_elsewhere[*]} (a checker, not an installer)")
    else
      deck_orphan_detail+=("$deck_list -- read by nothing at all, anywhere in the overlaid tree")
    fi
  done

  build_fail "guard 6.8: ${#deck_orphan_lists[@]} of ${#DECK_PACKAGE_LISTS[@]} package list(s) in configs/deck/ have NO CONSUMER. Nothing in builder/ or configs/airootfs/ reads them, so nothing installs them.

$(printf '    %s\n' "${deck_orphan_detail[@]}")

  A package list nothing reads is a comment. It can be written, reviewed,
  maintained, unit-tested and shipped, and the machine that boots off this ISO
  is exactly as if the file had never existed. That is not a theory: it is
  docs/findings/P32-steam-never-installed.md -- eleven green install steps,
  18/18 in the QEMU harness, and a Deck with no /usr/lib/steam showing a black
  panel, because the only thing that ever opened deck-fetch.packages was a
  dry-run checker.

  WHAT COUNTS as a consumer here: a non-comment reference to the list's name in
  builder/ (the container's own scripts -- the live pacstrap, the offline
  mirror's download list, the merge into omarchy-base.packages) or in
  configs/airootfs/ (what the live ISO carries and runs, the install
  orchestrator included).

  WHAT DOES NOT: a reference from another file in configs/deck/ -- that is
  where the lists' own pre-flight checkers live, and reading a list to ask a
  question about it installs nothing -- or a reference from iso/bin/build,
  which is this guard.

  Wire the list into something that installs it, or delete the list. Do not
  satisfy this by adding a reference from a checker."
fi

build_log "guard 6.8 OK: all ${#DECK_PACKAGE_LISTS[@]} package list(s) in configs/deck/ are read by something that installs -- ${deck_consumer_report[*]}"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.5 -- the payload audit (T5-fork-plan.md §6.5, specified in §5.4).
#
# WHY: docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17 and operator decision §5.25 #7. The dev Deck
# keeps /etc/sudoers.d/99-deck-testing on purpose -- the iterate-in-place SSH
# loop needs it, and §5.17 established that no narrower grant does the job --
# and the ISO must REFUSE to carry it. §5.4 is explicit that this FAILS THE
# BUILD. A warning here would be a comment with extra steps: the whole reason
# the file exists on the Deck is that it is convenient, which is exactly the
# pressure that gets a warning ignored.
#
# tools/iso-payload-audit.sh is the check itself (16 assertions of its own in
# test/unit/test-iso-payload-audit.sh). It runs on the HOST, not in the
# container: it sources src/deck-session.sh to share the blanket/passwordless
# predicate with `stage-audit-privileges` rather than reimplement it, so it
# needs this repo's tree, which the container never sees.
#
# Like guard 6.4 this is in two halves, and neither alone is honest.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PAYLOAD_AUDIT="$REPO_ROOT/tools/iso-payload-audit.sh"

# run_payload_audit <label> <root>... -- sets PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS, and returns
# it so callers can write `if ! run_payload_audit ...; then`.
#
# The status is captured HERE rather than at the call site because `if ! cmd`
# sets $? to the negated value: inside the `then` branch of `if ! cmd`, $? is 0
# no matter what cmd returned. A call site could therefore not tell exit 2
# (usage -- a bug in this wiring) from exit 1 (a real finding in the payload),
# and reporting the second as the first would send the reader hunting through
# the overlay for a file that is not there. Same reason verify_checkout_at_pin
# sets a global: the honest value has to survive the control flow.
PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=0
run_payload_audit() {
  local label=$1
  shift
  PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=0
  # A missing or unrunnable audit is a FAILURE, never a skipped check. In a
  # green build log "the audit found nothing" and "the audit never ran" look
  # identical, and this project has shipped that mistake before (docs/PROGRESS.md
  # §5.30c). 126/127 are the shell's own cannot-execute/not-found codes,
  # borrowed so they cannot collide with the audit's documented 0/1/2.
  if [[ ! -f $PAYLOAD_AUDIT ]]; then PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=127; return 127; fi
  if [[ ! -x $PAYLOAD_AUDIT ]]; then PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=126; return 126; fi
  # Named roots, so a green build says what was audited. The audit prints its
  # own denominator (files/roots/archives inspected) and that is not repeated.
  build_log "payload audit ($label): $*"
  bash "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT" "$@" || PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=$?
  return "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS"
}

# payload_audit_verdict <status> -- one sentence saying what a status MEANS.
# Shared by both halves so they cannot describe the same code differently.
payload_audit_verdict() {
  case $1 in
    1) printf '%s' "the audit FOUND a sudoers grant giving blanket root with NO password -- its own WARNING lines above name the file and the offending line" ;;
    2) printf '%s' "the audit exited 2, which is its USAGE code. That is a bug in this script's wiring (no roots passed, or a path that is not a directory), not a finding about the payload -- fix the call, do not delete it" ;;
    126) printf '%s' "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT exists but is not executable, so the audit did not run" ;;
    127) printf '%s' "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT does not exist, so the audit did not run" ;;
    *) printf '%s' "the audit exited $1, which is none of its documented codes (0 clean, 1 finding, 2 usage). An unknown status is a failure, not a pass" ;;
  esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.5a -- before Docker, beside 6.4a, and for the same reason: it costs a
# find(1) and it refuses the build before six gigabytes of downloads.
#
# Two roots, answering different questions:
#
#   $ISO_ROOT/overlay      what this fork ADDS. The likeliest route for the
#                          accident is a file copied out of src/ or off the dev
#                          Deck, where that grant is deliberate.
#   $SCRATCH_SRC/configs   the overlaid, PATCHED airootfs source -- upstream's
#                          tree plus our rsync (step 4) plus our patches
#                          (step 5), i.e. what mkarchiso is about to be handed.
#
# ⚠️ The second root is NOT §5.4's "assembled airootfs work dir", and saying so
# matters more than the guard reading tidily. mkarchiso's real work dir is
# built inside the container and is never bind-mounted out, so nothing on this
# host can read it. $SCRATCH_SRC/configs is the closest thing that exists here:
# it is the exact input to that assembly, so it covers everything WE put in the
# live filesystem -- but not a drop-in that arrives from a package during the
# airootfs pacstrap. That case belongs to 6.5b, which is why there are two.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if ! run_payload_audit "overlay + overlaid airootfs source" \
  "$ISO_ROOT/overlay" "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs"; then
  build_fail "guard 6.5a: $(payload_audit_verdict "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS")

  roots audited
    $ISO_ROOT/overlay        (what this fork adds)
    $SCRATCH_SRC/configs     (upstream + overlay + patches, as handed to mkarchiso)

  docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §5.4: /etc/sudoers.d/99-deck-testing is deliberate
  on the dev Deck (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17, operator decision §5.25 #7) and an
  ISO that ships one is not a product. Take it out of the payload -- do not
  weaken the audit."
fi

build_log "guard 6.5a OK: no blanket passwordless root in the overlay or in the airootfs source handed to mkarchiso"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. The build. Mirrors iso/upstream/bin/omarchy-iso-make's docker invocation
#    of builder/build-iso.sh, minus the two things guard 6.3 forbids (see the
#    file header) and minus the interactive "boot it?" prompt at the end.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1 || build_fail "docker is required and not on PATH"

NETWORK_ARGS=(--network host)
if [[ ${OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_NO_NETWORK_HOST:-} == 1 ]]; then
  NETWORK_ARGS=()
  build_log "OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_NO_NETWORK_HOST=1: using Docker's default bridge network (slower on this dev machine -- docs/PROGRESS.md §7)"
else
  build_log "using --network host (docs/PROGRESS.md §7: the default bridge is throttled to ~2 KB/s here)"
fi

DOCKER_ARGS=(
  --rm
  --privileged
  "${NETWORK_ARGS[@]}"
  -e "OMARCHY_ISO_REF=$BUILD_ISO_REF"
  -e "OMARCHY_MIRROR=$BUILD_MIRROR"
  -e "OMARCHY_INSTALL_DEBUG=${OMARCHY_INSTALL_DEBUG:-}"
  # Stated rather than re-derived from the ref inside the container, so the
  # names guard 6.4b looks for on the way out are the names that were built.
  -e "OMARCHY_RUNTIME_PACKAGE=$RUNTIME_PACKAGE"
  -e "OMARCHY_SETTINGS_PACKAGE=$SETTINGS_PACKAGE"
  -e "OMARCHY_NVIM_PACKAGE=$NVIM_PACKAGE"
  -e "HOST_UID=$(id -u)"
  -e "HOST_GID=$(id -g)"
  -v "$RELEASE_DIR/:/out/"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/builder/build-iso.sh:/build-iso.sh:ro"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/archiso:/archiso:ro"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/builder:/builder:ro"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs:/configs:ro"
  # --local-source (step 2b): both mounts, always together.
  -v "$RUNTIME_SRC:/omarchy-source:ro"
  -v "$PKGS_SRC:/omarchy-pkgs:ro"
  # Guard 6.3: scratch caches, never the host's.
  -v "$PACMAN_CACHE_DIR:/var/cache/pacman/pkg"
  -v "$OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR:/var/cache/airootfs/var/cache/omarchy"
)

build_log "starting docker build (this downloads hundreds of packages; can take a long time on first run)..."
docker run "${DOCKER_ARGS[@]}" archlinux/archlinux:latest /build-iso.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Locate the result. Naming happens further down, AFTER guard 6.4b -- a
#    rejected ISO must not be handed a finished-looking name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

shopt -s nullglob
built_isos=("$RELEASE_DIR"/*.iso)
shopt -u nullglob
[[ ${#built_isos[@]} -ge 1 ]] ||
  build_fail "docker build reported success but no .iso appeared in $RELEASE_DIR"

# Newest first; mkarchiso writes exactly one per run, but be explicit rather
# than assume. find -printf avoids ls's non-alphanumeric-filename footguns.
latest_iso=$(find "${built_isos[@]}" -maxdepth 0 -printf '%T@ %p\n' | sort -rn | head -n1 | cut -d' ' -f2-)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.4b -- the same coupling, re-asked of the artifact that will ship.
#
# 6.4a read the runtime CHECKOUT and our PKGBUILD (a recipe); this reads the
# .pkg.tar.zst files the build actually produced, which is what the target
# installs. They can differ: omarchy-dev's PKGBUILD ships bin/* except
# omarchy-debug, omarchy-debug-idle and omarchy-upload-log, which
# omarchy-settings-dev ships instead -- so the honest provider set is the
# UNION of the packages, and only the packages know it.
#
# The packages are readable from here because the container writes the
# offline mirror into a bind mount (build-iso.sh's
# /var/cache/airootfs/var/cache/omarchy). Yes, this runs after the expensive
# part -- 6.4a is the cheap early gate. What this one buys is the difference
# between shipping an ISO that dies at phase 5 and not shipping one at all.
#
# It also answers the question 6.4a cannot: DID THE PIN TAKE EFFECT? Both
# PKGBUILDs compute pkgver as <base>.r<count>.g<short sha> from the source
# tree's git history, so a package version that does not carry iso/RUNTIME's
# short sha means the offline mirror holds a channel build, not ours --
# --local-source silently not having applied is precisely the failure this
# slice exists to prevent, and it is invisible in a green build log.
#
# ✅ THE SYMMETRY 6.4a GREW FIRST IS NOW HERE. This half used to ask only
# omarchy-dev + omarchy-settings-dev, so the day a literal
# /usr/bin/omarchy-deck-* appeared in the orchestrator, 6.4a passed and this
# guard failed -- after Docker, with the wrong diagnosis ("check
# pkgbuilds/omarchy-dev's package() exclusion list"), i.e. the same
# wrong-diagnosis bug 6.4a had just been fixed for, six gigabytes later. It now
# reads OUR built packages out of the same offline mirror, by the same rule
# (.PKGINFO is the authority on which package a file is; usr/bin/* members are
# the provider set), and keeps the two sets separate in every message.
#
# 🔴 AN ABSENT PACKAGE OF OURS IS A REJECTION, NOT A QUIET PASS. The set of
# packages we owe is derived above from configs/deck/pkgbuilds/*/PKGBUILD in the
# OVERLAID tree -- the same single declaration the patched build-iso.sh builds
# from (seam S4, "CONSUMER 1"). So "the mirror has no omarchy-deck" is not an
# absence of expectation, it is an expectation that went unmet: the local
# package build did not run. Passing on it would be this project's recurring
# failure -- "found nothing" reported as "found no problems"
# (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.30c) -- and the consequence is concrete, because
# omarchy-deck is listed in deck-install.packages: pacstrap fails on a target
# the offline mirror does not carry, ~1000 packages into an install, on a
# device with no terminal.
#
# The version question is asked of our packages too, but differently, because
# the pin is a different one. omarchy-dev's version must carry iso/RUNTIME's
# sha; omarchy-deck's pkgver is hand-set in a commit (its PKGBUILD forbids a
# derived one, since /configs is a bind mount with no .git), so the pin is the
# recipe itself. The offline mirror is PERSISTENT across builds (guard 6.3), so
# without this a build whose deck-packages.patch stopped applying would silently
# ship the PREVIOUS run's omarchy-deck -- present, readable, and stale.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR="$OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR/mirror/offline"

reject_iso() {
  if [[ -f $latest_iso ]]; then
    mv -f "$latest_iso" "$latest_iso.rejected"
    build_log "renamed the built ISO to $(basename -- "$latest_iso").rejected -- it must not be flashed, and a later run's *.iso glob must not pick it up"
  fi
  build_fail "$1"
}

# inspect_package <file> -- sets PKG_NAME, PKG_VER, PKG_BINS.
PKG_NAME=""
PKG_VER=""
PKG_BINS=()
inspect_package() {
  local file=$1 pkginfo listing
  # Captured, not piped: bsdtar failing mid-read inside a process
  # substitution would look exactly like a package with no members.
  pkginfo=$(bsdtar -xOf "$file" .PKGINFO) ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: could not read .PKGINFO from $file"
  listing=$(bsdtar -tf "$file") ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: could not list the members of $file"
  PKG_NAME=$(printf '%s\n' "$pkginfo" | sed -n 's/^pkgname = //p' | head -n1)
  PKG_VER=$(printf '%s\n' "$pkginfo" | sed -n 's/^pkgver = //p' | head -n1)
  [[ -n $PKG_NAME && -n $PKG_VER ]] ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: $file has no pkgname/pkgver in .PKGINFO"
  mapfile -t PKG_BINS < <(printf '%s\n' "$listing" | sed -n 's|^usr/bin/\(..*\)$|\1|p' | sort -u)
}

command -v bsdtar >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b needs bsdtar (libarchive) to read the built packages and it is not on PATH. Refusing to skip the check that reads the artifact -- install libarchive and re-run."
[[ -d $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR ]] ||
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b: no offline mirror at $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR after a successful build. The container should have written the locally built packages there."

# find_mirror_package <pkgname> -- sets MIRROR_PKG_FILE, MIRROR_PKG_VER and
# MIRROR_PKG_BINS for the one package of that name in the offline mirror, or
# leaves MIRROR_PKG_FILE empty if there is none. Rejects on ambiguity: two
# archives for one name means the ISO's contents cannot be attributed to
# either, which is not a state any check downstream can reason about.
MIRROR_PKG_FILE=""
MIRROR_PKG_VER=""
MIRROR_PKG_BINS=()
find_mirror_package() {
  local pkg_name=$1 candidate
  MIRROR_PKG_FILE=""
  MIRROR_PKG_VER=""
  MIRROR_PKG_BINS=()
  shopt -s nullglob
  local candidates=("$OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR/$pkg_name-"*.pkg.tar.*)
  shopt -u nullglob
  for candidate in "${candidates[@]}"; do
    [[ $candidate == *.sig ]] && continue
    inspect_package "$candidate"
    # The glob is a prefix match; .PKGINFO is the authority on which package
    # this file actually is.
    [[ $PKG_NAME == "$pkg_name" ]] || continue
    [[ -z $MIRROR_PKG_FILE ]] ||
      reject_iso "guard 6.4b: more than one $pkg_name package in $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR. Cannot tell which one the ISO carries, so cannot verify either."
    MIRROR_PKG_FILE=$candidate
    MIRROR_PKG_VER=$PKG_VER
    MIRROR_PKG_BINS=("${PKG_BINS[@]}")
  done
}

# --- provider set 1: the pinned runtime's own packages ---------------------

RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS=()
for pkg_name in "$RUNTIME_PACKAGE" "$SETTINGS_PACKAGE"; do
  find_mirror_package "$pkg_name"

  [[ -n $MIRROR_PKG_FILE ]] ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: no $pkg_name package in $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR. --local-source should have built one from $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT; its absence means the local build did not run and the ISO's runtime came from the '$BUILD_MIRROR' channel instead -- an unpinned runtime, which is the whole thing this slice removes."

  case $MIRROR_PKG_VER in
    *".g$RUNTIME_SHORT"*) ;;
    *) reject_iso "guard 6.4b: $pkg_name is version '$MIRROR_PKG_VER', which does not carry iso/RUNTIME's commit ($RUNTIME_SHORT). Both PKGBUILDs derive pkgver from the source tree's git history, so this package was NOT built from $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD. The runtime pin did not take effect." ;;
  esac
  RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS+=("${MIRROR_PKG_BINS[@]}")
  build_log "guard 6.4b: $pkg_name $MIRROR_PKG_VER built from $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT (${#MIRROR_PKG_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries)"
done

(( ${#RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} > 0 )) ||
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b: the built packages ship no usr/bin members at all. That is not a runtime; refusing to report a pass."

# --- provider set 2: the packages WE build (the symmetric half) ------------
#
# Same mirror, same rule, separate list -- see the block header for why an
# absence here is a rejection and why the version question is asked against
# the recipe rather than against iso/RUNTIME.

DECK_BUILT_BINS=()
DECK_BUILT_NOTE=""
if (( ${#DECK_PACKAGE_NAMES[@]} == 0 )); then
  DECK_BUILT_NOTE="the overlaid tree declares no configs/deck/pkgbuilds/*/PKGBUILD, so this build owes no package of ours and the provider set is the runtime's alone"
  build_log "guard 6.4b: $DECK_BUILT_NOTE"
else
  for pkg_name in "${DECK_PACKAGE_NAMES[@]}"; do
    # The recipe's hand-set version, read from the copy the container built
    # from. Under-deriving must not be quiet (6.4a's lesson): a pkgver this
    # cannot read is refused rather than skipped, because a skipped version
    # check is indistinguishable from a passing one in a green log.
    recipe_pkgver=$(sed -n 's/^pkgver=//p' "$DECK_PKGBUILDS_ROOT/$pkg_name/PKGBUILD" | head -n1)
    case $recipe_pkgver in
      ""|*'$'*|*'`'*|*'('*)
        reject_iso "guard 6.4b: could not read a literal pkgver= out of configs/deck/pkgbuilds/$pkg_name/PKGBUILD (got '$recipe_pkgver').

  That file's own rules forbid a derived pkgver -- /configs is a read-only bind
  mount with no .git in it, and a version that moves per build breaks the
  'exactly one artifact per name' check in the patched build-iso.sh. Without a
  literal version here there is nothing to compare the built package against,
  and the offline mirror is persistent across builds, so a stale artifact from
  a previous run would pass unnoticed. Set pkgver by hand, in a commit." ;;
    esac

    find_mirror_package "$pkg_name"

    [[ -n $MIRROR_PKG_FILE ]] ||
      reject_iso "guard 6.4b: no $pkg_name package in $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR, and this build owed one.

  configs/deck/pkgbuilds/$pkg_name/PKGBUILD exists in the overlaid tree, and
  the patched builder/build-iso.sh builds every such directory straight into
  this mirror (seam S4). So its absence after a SUCCESSFUL container run means
  the local package build did not run at all -- deck-packages.patch did not
  reach this build, or the mirror was pruned after the package was placed.

  This is a defect, not an exemption. Reporting it as a pass would be
  'found nothing' recorded as 'found no problems'. The ISO would install with
  no $pkg_name on the target -- and $pkg_name is named in
  configs/deck/deck-install.packages, so pacstrap fails on a package the
  offline mirror does not carry, ~1000 packages into an install, on a device
  with no terminal."

    case $MIRROR_PKG_VER in
      "$recipe_pkgver"|"$recipe_pkgver"-*|*:"$recipe_pkgver"|*:"$recipe_pkgver"-*) ;;
      *) reject_iso "guard 6.4b: $pkg_name in $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR is version '$MIRROR_PKG_VER', but configs/deck/pkgbuilds/$pkg_name/PKGBUILD says pkgver=$recipe_pkgver.

  The offline mirror is a persistent bind mount across builds (guard 6.3), so
  the likeliest cause is that THIS build did not produce the package and a
  previous run's artifact is still sitting there. The ISO would ship a
  $pkg_name built from a recipe nobody in this build ever read." ;;
    esac

    DECK_BUILT_BINS+=("${MIRROR_PKG_BINS[@]}")
    build_log "guard 6.4b: $pkg_name $MIRROR_PKG_VER built from configs/deck/pkgbuilds/$pkg_name (${#MIRROR_PKG_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries)"
  done
  DECK_BUILT_NOTE="our own packages (${DECK_PACKAGE_NAMES[*]}) ship ${#DECK_BUILT_BINS[@]} /usr/bin entries between them"
  (( ${#DECK_BUILT_BINS[@]} == 0 )) ||
    DECK_BUILT_NOTE="$DECK_BUILT_NOTE: ${DECK_BUILT_BINS[*]}"
fi

# --- the union. Not a bypass: a name neither set covers still rejects. ------

missing_bins=()
deck_built_supplied=()
for want in "${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]}"; do
  found=0
  for have in "${RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS[@]}"; do
    [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; break; }
  done
  if (( ! found )); then
    for have in "${DECK_BUILT_BINS[@]}"; do
      [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; deck_built_supplied+=("$want"); break; }
    done
  fi
  (( found )) || missing_bins+=("$want")
done

if (( ${#missing_bins[@]} > 0 )); then
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b: the ISO's orchestrator calls binaries the packages it carries do not contain.

  installer     $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head (iso/UPSTREAM)
  runtime       $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD (iso/RUNTIME)
  runtime pkgs  $RUNTIME_PACKAGE + $SETTINGS_PACKAGE (${#RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries between them)
  our packages  $DECK_BUILT_NOTE

  Shipped by neither provider, in the artifacts this build produced:
$(printf '    /usr/bin/%s\n' "${missing_bins[@]}")
  Two different bugs wear this shape, and the fix is not the same one:

    * THE PINNED RUNTIME'S PACKAGES DO NOT SHIP IT. Guard 6.4a passed on the
      checkout, so the file IS in bin/ and the PKGBUILD did not install it --
      check pkgbuilds/$RUNTIME_PACKAGE's package() exclusion list in the pkgs
      checkout ($PKGS_SRC).

    * OUR PACKAGE DOES NOT SHIP IT EITHER. If the name is one of ours, the pins
      are both fine: the built package on the 'our packages' line above does not
      contain usr/bin/<name>. 6.4a reads the PKGBUILD's package() body and this
      half reads the ARCHIVE, so a name that satisfied 6.4a and fails here means
      the recipe and the artifact disagree -- the install line was changed,
      conditioned, or never ran.

  Left alone, either one fails at 'Configuring system', phase 5 of 14, AFTER
  partitioning, LUKS, pacstrap and ~1200 packages."
fi

if (( ${#deck_built_supplied[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_log "guard 6.4b OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries are present in the packages this ISO carries -- ${#deck_built_supplied[@]} from our own built packages (${deck_built_supplied[*]}), the rest from $RUNTIME_PACKAGE + $SETTINGS_PACKAGE (${#RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries)"
else
  build_log "guard 6.4b OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries are present in the packages this ISO carries -- all from $RUNTIME_PACKAGE + $SETTINGS_PACKAGE (${#RUNTIME_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries); $DECK_BUILT_NOTE"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.5b -- the payload audit, re-asked of what the build actually produced.
#
# 6.5a read TREES. This reads the offline mirror, which is packages, and that
# is the half that matters most: a sudoers drop-in shipped INSIDE a
# .pkg.tar.zst lands on the installed system exactly like one baked into
# airootfs, and no tree scan would ever see it. It is also the route our own
# future omarchy-deck package would take (T5-fork-plan.md §5.4 names that
# package as one of the four roots), so this is the one place the audit can
# catch us shipping the dev Deck's grant by way of our own packaging.
#
# The mirror is readable from the host because build-iso.sh writes it into a
# bind mount (/var/cache/airootfs/var/cache/omarchy) -- the same reason 6.4b
# can read the built packages at all.
#
# ⚠️ It is the slowest check in this script: the audit opens every
# *.pkg.tar.* in the mirror with bsdtar, which on a full build is ~1250
# archives. That is why it runs once, here, rather than per package -- and why
# 6.5a is the cheap half that runs before Docker.
#
# On any failure this goes through reject_iso, not build_fail: the ISO already
# exists on disk by this point, and an image that would ship blanket
# passwordless root must not keep a name that can be flashed or picked up by a
# later run's *.iso glob.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if ! run_payload_audit "offline mirror" "$OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR"; then
  reject_iso "guard 6.5b: $(payload_audit_verdict "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS")

  root audited
    $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR   (the packages this ISO carries)

  A drop-in inside a package installs exactly like one baked into airootfs, so
  this is not a lesser finding than 6.5a's -- it is the likelier one, and the
  route our own omarchy-deck package would take (T5-fork-plan.md §5.4,
  docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17). The ISO has been renamed .rejected above."
fi

build_log "guard 6.5b OK: no blanket passwordless root in any package in the offline mirror"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Name and report the result. Matches omarchy-iso-make's own
#    <name>-<ref>.iso convention so the artifact is unambiguous about which
#    ref/mirror pair produced it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

case $latest_iso in
  *-"$BUILD_ISO_REF".iso) final_iso=$latest_iso ;;
  *) final_iso="${latest_iso%.*}-$BUILD_ISO_REF.iso"; mv -f "$latest_iso" "$final_iso" ;;
esac

# THE SIZE NUMBER (T5-iso-and-payload.md §4, T5g). Printed in one fixed,
# machine-readable shape because something else parses it: the size gate in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml reads exactly
#
#     [iso-build] iso size: <bytes> bytes
#
# out of this run's log, and test/unit/test-ci-workflow.sh asserts that the
# gate's expression still matches the line this line produces. Change the
# format here and that suite goes red in seconds -- which is the point, since
# the alternative is a gate that silently matches nothing and therefore
# silently passes everything.
#
# stat, not `du` or `ls`: the apparent byte count is what a USB target and a
# release note care about, and it is the number docs/findings/T5a-parity.md
# and docs/PROGRESS.md record for every image measured so far.
iso_bytes=$(stat -c '%s' "$final_iso") ||
  build_fail "built $final_iso but could not stat it to record its size"
# Integer arithmetic and printf, both bash builtins, rather than awk or bc: by
# this point the script has already proved it can build an ISO, and failing at
# the last line for want of a formatting tool would be absurd.
iso_gib_hundredths=$(( iso_bytes * 100 / 1073741824 ))
printf -v iso_gib '%d.%02d' $(( iso_gib_hundredths / 100 )) $(( iso_gib_hundredths % 100 ))
build_log "iso size: $iso_bytes bytes ($iso_gib GiB)"

build_log "build complete: $final_iso"
build_log "$(du -h "$final_iso" | cut -f1) -- $(sha256sum "$final_iso")"
